// GENERATED by scripts/build-chat-kb.mjs from public pages, tutorials and docs/ai-assistant-kb.md. Do not edit by hand: change the source, then run `node scripts/build-chat-kb.mjs`.
// 262 chunks. Public text only; the assistant strips dollar figures and dates from it before use.
export const KB_CHUNKS = [{"title":"Running a contest: the steps (1 of 2)","url":"/help#run","text":"Open the Add page (signed in, tap the account circle at the top right and choose Add an event, home or contest; not signed in, tap the three dots and choose Add) and choose Contest (or go to /add-contest). The contest wizard asks for the contest name, who is running it and the city, a welcome message (already written for you), an optional website, and the dates (filled in for you: the last day to enter, and when voting starts and ends). Then you choose how to set it up. Quick start (recommended) keeps the defaults: public voting, four categories, you approve each entry, and we draft the official rules; it takes about a minute. Customize everything adds screens for judges, prizes, categories, vote counts and your own rules. Everything can be changed later from your dashboard."},{"title":"Running a contest: the steps (2 of 2)","url":"/help#run","text":"The last screen is Review, where you check everything and tap Create contest. You sign in on that screen if you have not already, and your draft is kept while you sign in, whether you use Google or an emailed code. When you finish, the contest normally goes live right away and appears on the map as an event; if it is held for a quick check, we email you when it is approved, and your link and QR code already work. Each contest has its own page at /c/<name> (the page a QR code opens). The organizer dashboard is at /c/<name>/manage: approve or decline entries, invite judges, pause or start voting, hide or show vote counts, announce winners, copy the link, and print a QR poster. An organizer can create up to 3 contests per account per day."},{"title":"Adding your home to the map (no contest needed) (1 of 3)","url":"/add-house","text":"You do not need a contest to put your home on the map. Adding a home, also called showing your lights or listing your display, is free and works on its own. Go to the Add page (signed in: account circle at the top right, then Add an event, home or contest; not signed in: the three dots, then Add) and choose Home (or open /add-house). Sign in first with Google or an emailed code, because every listing belongs to the account that adds it."},{"title":"Adding your home to the map (no contest needed) (2 of 3)","url":"/add-house","text":"The form asks: who is posting (a homeowner or a professional installer), the name to show, which season the display is for, a display name and a few sentences about it, the address (city, street and 5-digit ZIP) so the pin can go on the map, and optional details such as when the lights are on, drive or walk by, parking, accessibility, radio station and tips. Photos are optional: up to 8, and you can add them later by email. On the last screen you check everything and choose Put it on the map; the home normally appears right away, and on a very busy day a new home may wait briefly for review. You can ask us to change or remove it at any time. If the map already shows an automatic listing for your address, open it and choose Is this your home?"},{"title":"Adding your home to the map (no contest needed) (3 of 3)","url":"/add-house","text":"to claim it; your listing usually goes live at once and replaces the automatic one, and if it is held for a quick check it goes live once the site owner has looked it over. Entering a contest is a separate, optional step: a contest asks you to be 18 or older, add photos and accept that contest's rules."},{"title":"Entering a home in a contest","url":"/help#enter","text":"To enter, open the contest page and choose Enter. You must sign in, add photos, the home's address and a name, and accept the contest's official rules. You must be 18 or older to enter a home. A home does not pick a category: it competes in every category. If the organizer approves entries by hand, your photos stay private until the organizer approves your entry; if entries are approved automatically, your home appears right away. You can edit or withdraw your entry from your profile while the contest allows it."},{"title":"Voting in a contest","url":"/help#vote","text":"Voting is free and needs a sign-in. Every approved home is listed in every voting category (for example Best Overall or Most Creative). Each voter picks one favorite home in each category. Voting again in a category moves your vote. Some contests give one vote in total instead; the contest page says which. Nobody can vote for their own home. Voting opens on the contest's start date (or when the organizer starts it early) and stops when the winners are announced. Fake, automated or traded votes are not allowed and may be voided. Votes are never sold."},{"title":"Categories, winners and ties","url":"/help#run","text":"The organizer defines the voting categories. Best Overall is an ordinary category like any other. There is no computed overall winner. For a single overall winner, use one category. Winners are the top home in each category. Vote counts are hidden until winners are announced unless the organizer chooses to show them. An organizer may choose to give each home only one win. Tied homes share the win. Each contest's organizer writes its official rules, decides who wins, and provides any prize."},{"title":"How an organizer opens the contest dashboard","url":"/contests#mine","text":"The organizer dashboard is where you run your contest: approve entries, add co-organizers and judges, change details, share the link and announce winners. To open it, sign in with the account that created the contest (or the email a co-organizer was invited at). Then tap the account circle at the top right and choose Your contests. That opens your contests (the address is /contests#mine); tap Manage on the contest. You can also open your contest's own page while signed in and tap Manage this contest. The dashboard address is /c/<name>/manage, where <name> is the contest's name in its web address. Inside the dashboard the sections are Overview, Entries, Results, Judges, Details, Promote, Team and Sponsors. On a phone they are in the bar at the bottom, and More shows the rest."},{"title":"Judges and how judging works (1 of 2)","url":"/help/judge-a-contest","text":"A contest can be public voting only (People's Choice), judges only (Judges' Choice), or both. The organizer adds judges by email from the dashboard. Each judge gets an email with a private link to the judges' ballot at /c/<name>/judge and signs in with the email they were invited at. Judges vote on the judges' ballot, not in the public vote. The organizer chooses one of two methods. Pick: each judge picks one favorite home in every category. Score: each judge scores every home from 1 to 10 in every category; there is no separate rubric or criteria list. When 5 or more judges score a home, the highest and lowest score for it are dropped before averaging (a trimmed average), so one very generous or very harsh judge cannot swing it."},{"title":"Judges and how judging works (2 of 2)","url":"/help/judge-a-contest","text":"A judge cannot judge their own home, and a judge can mark a home as a conflict and skip it; skipped homes do not count for that judge. Judging happens during the contest's voting dates."},{"title":"How votes, judge results and ties decide winners (1 of 2)","url":"/help/how-winners-are-chosen","text":"Winners are worked out in each category separately. People's Choice goes to the home with the most public votes in that category. Judges' Choice goes to the home with the most judge picks, or the highest judge score. They are separate awards: public votes and judge scores are not added together. When a contest uses both, the judges' result is used only to separate homes that are tied on public votes. Tied homes that remain tied share the win. Votes the organizer voided do not count. A home the organizer flagged to be disqualified stays on the ballot but cannot win. If the organizer chose one win per home, a home that wins one category cannot win a later one."},{"title":"How votes, judge results and ties decide winners (2 of 2)","url":"/help/how-winners-are-chosen","text":"The organizer announces the winners; the contest's organizer writes the official rules and decides who wins, and any prize comes from the organizer."},{"title":"Prizes and organizer fairness tools","url":"/help#run","text":"Organizers can set a prize per award. To help keep voting fair, organizers can review the votes in their contest, void a vote that looks fake (and restore it if it was a mistake), flag a home to be disqualified at close, and download the results as a spreadsheet. These decisions belong to the organizer; questions about a specific decision go to the organizer. Disputes and eligibility edge cases are for the organizer under our Terms."},{"title":"Who can vote and how often","url":"/help/who-can-vote","text":"Anyone with an account can vote in a contest that has public voting; voting is free. You sign in with Google or an emailed code, so your vote belongs to your account (your verified email address), not to a device. You get one vote per category: pick one favorite home in each category, and the contest page says if that contest gives one vote in total instead. You cannot vote for your own home. A judge in the contest votes on the judges' ballot instead of the public vote. Voting is open only between the contest's voting dates, or when the organizer starts it early, and the organizer can pause it. It closes when the winners are announced."},{"title":"Changing a vote","url":"/help/can-i-change-my-vote","text":"While voting is open you can change your vote: open the category and tap Move my vote here on the home you now prefer, and your vote moves to it (the page says \"vote moved\"). Judges can change their scores the same way until voting closes. Choosing the home you already picked says it is already your vote. There is no separate button to withdraw a vote without choosing another home. After the winners are announced votes are locked."},{"title":"My vote did not count or I cannot vote","url":"/help/i-cannot-vote","text":"Common reasons: you are not signed in; voting is not open yet, is paused, or has ended; the winners were already announced; you tried to vote for your own home; you are a judge in this contest (use the judges' ballot); the contest is judges only; or you voted again in the same category, which moves your earlier vote instead of adding a second one. Organizers can also void a vote that looks fake. If none of these fit, email info@socallights.app and say which contest it was; please do not include personal information."},{"title":"Vote counts, fairness and fraud protection","url":"/help#vote","text":"Vote counts are hidden until the winners are announced unless the organizer chooses to show them. To protect fairness, voting needs a verified sign-in, each account gets one vote per category, nobody can vote for their own home, and organizers can review and void votes that look fake. Fake, automated or traded votes are not allowed, and votes are never sold. We do not share how fake votes are detected. Paying for anything never gives anyone votes or changes a result."},{"title":"Announcing winners and the results page","url":"/past","text":"When the organizer announces winners, the results are saved as a fixed snapshot and votes are locked. Every entrant is emailed, and winners get a congratulations email. The permanent results page is at /past/<name>, and /past lists every announced contest. Winners can show a crown badge on their listings; each person can hide badges from the Badges tab on their profile."},{"title":"Organizer-added homes","url":"/help#run","text":"For cities that collect resident entries on their own form or spreadsheet, the organizer or a co-organizer can add homes themselves: on the dashboard, open the Entries tab and choose Add homes yourself. Paste one home per line (street address, optionally with city, ZIP and a name). Choose Check addresses first to see which were found, tick the box confirming the residents agreed to have their address shown on the public map, then add them. Up to 10 homes can be added at a time. Homes that cannot be found are not added. Added homes have no photo and no resident account. This can be done before voting starts. A mistake can be removed from the entries list before the home has votes."},{"title":"Unpublish a contest and publish it again","url":"/help#run","text":"The person who created a contest can unpublish it from the dashboard: Overview, Controls, Contest page, Unpublish (two taps). That takes it off the map and hides its page, but keeps the entries, votes, judges and sponsors. The creator can use Publish it again to put it back. Co-organizers cannot unpublish. If our team unpublished a contest, only our team can put it back."},{"title":"Contest passes: how they work (1 of 2)","url":"/contest-pass","text":"Running a contest needs a contest pass, starting with the first contest. The organizer chooses a pass before the contest goes live: a single contest, up to 5 contests or up to 20 contests in a 12-month window (the total counts every contest in the window). The window starts the day the pass is bought, or the day the organization's first contest goes live if that comes first. Upgrading inside the window costs only the difference. When the window ends, the next contest needs a new pass. Contests that were already live or announced before passes began are not affected. More than 20: contact us. Some organizations, such as a founding partner, are given contests for a period and then see \"Founding partner: free through\" a date instead of a price."},{"title":"Contest passes: how they work (2 of 2)","url":"/contest-pass","text":"A pass is one payment on Stripe's secure page and never renews by itself. You can buy ahead of time on the contest pass page, or the contest wizard offers it before a new contest goes live. Passes only count contests: they do not change entries, features or results. Entering and voting stay free, and payment never affects who wins."},{"title":"Sponsors of a contest","url":"/help#sponsors","text":"An organizer can record a sponsor who paid them directly. Sponsorship money goes to the organizer, not to SoCal Lights. A sponsor appears on the contest page only when the organizer has confirmed the payment (or an in-kind gift), has the sponsor's permission, and chooses to show it. The contest page then shows the sponsor's logo, name and link, labelled Sponsor, with the line that sponsors support the contest but do not influence who wins. Organizers can also share a private sponsor invite link (/s/<code>): a sponsor opens it without an account, fills in a short form, and nothing is shown on the contest page until the organizer confirms the payment and approves it. The organizer can turn the link off or make a new one. Never pay anyone you do not know."},{"title":"Featuring a home after a contest","url":"/feature","text":"After winners are announced, the owner of a home that finished first, second or third in any category can pay to feature it for 30 days, from the Feature page. Paying never changes a contest result. The price is on the Pricing page."},{"title":"Where to find Add, Contests, Tutorials and Help","url":"/help","text":"The map has two buttons at the top right: the account circle and the three dots. When you are signed in, Add (called Add an event, home or contest), Your contests, Tutorials and Help are in the account circle menu: tap the account circle at the top right. That menu also has Your profile and Sign out. When you are not signed in, Add, Contests, Tutorials and Help are in the three dots menu instead, and the account circle asks you to sign in. The three dots menu always has the Weekly lights email, Promote your event, Browse by city, Privacy and terms, and a way to email us."},{"title":"Accounts and sign-in","url":"/help#account","text":"You never need an account to browse the map, plan a night in Favorites or sign up for the weekly email. You sign in to add an event or a home, and to run, enter or vote in a contest, because each listing, contest and vote belongs to your account. There are no passwords: sign in with Google or with a 6-digit code emailed to you. Both reach the same account for the same email address. A sign-in lasts up to 30 days and a code lasts 10 minutes. You can delete your account from your profile, Account tab."},{"title":"Favorites: plan a night of lights and open a route (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#night","text":"The Favorites tab (heart icon, next to Places and Shop) is where you plan a night. Steps: tap the heart on every place you want to visit; open the Favorites tab; check your stops, which are put in a sensible driving order with the miles and time between them (\"Start here\", then \"from\" the previous stop); choose where to Start (My location, First stop or a city) and your maps app (Apple Maps, Google Maps or Waze; the change button next to Opens in switches it, and phones ask the first time and remember your choice); then tap Route these. Waze takes one stop at a time, so it starts with the first stop, and phones also show a Send to car button on each stop. The Route these button is also on the map itself: in the top bar on a computer, just above the list on a phone."},{"title":"Favorites: plan a night of lights and open a route (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#night","text":"Your maps app takes about 9 stops at a time, so a longer night is split into routes of 9 or fewer and each route starts where the last one ended (the button shows how many routes). Tick off stops as you go. Tap the share icon to send your favorites to family or friends, who open the same list on their phone. A question-mark button beside the stop count shows or hides a short three-step guide: tap the heart, check your stops, tap Route these. Favorites are saved on your own device only."},{"title":"Remove my home from the map","url":"/remove-home","text":"If your home is on SoCal Lights and you would rather it were not, open the Remove my home page (socallights.app/remove-home). You do not need an account. Enter which home (the listing id from its page, or the street address) and your email so we can confirm and tell you when it is removed, add anything else we should know if you like, and tap Send my request. We email you when it is done. We use your email only to answer this request. You can also write to info@socallights.app."},{"title":"Promote your event: how to pay for a Featured pin","url":"/feature","text":"Listing an event is always free; Featured placement is an optional, one-time payment with no subscription. To feature an event: open the three-dot menu and tap Promote your event, or go to the Feature page. Pick a plan: every plan includes the gold Featured pin on the map; the banner adds a Featured pill in the strip at the top of the map list, and Top of the list puts your event first when people browse your area. Choose your event from your list (a home that finished first, second or third in an announced contest can be featured the same way), check the start date, end date and total, and tap Pay on Stripe, where you pay by card or Cash App Pay. Featured events carry a visible Featured badge and still have to be accurate. Plan prices are on the Pricing page."},{"title":"Event Partner: a plan for organizations with several events (1 of 2)","url":"/partner","text":"Listing an event is always free and stays free. Event Partner is an optional plan for cities, chambers of commerce, nonprofits and event producers: one payment per season covers up to 10 of the organization's events in that season. Each covered event gets the Featured pin (a gold-ringed pin on the map and a Featured badge) for its run window, and an Event Partner badge on its page. Featuring opens 30 days before an event starts and runs to the event's last day or the end of the season, whichever comes first (the Christmas lights season ends January 6). To use it, go to the Event Partner page, sign in with the email you use for your organization (an address at the organization's own website is matched to it automatically), pay once on Stripe, then choose Use the plan next to each event."},{"title":"Event Partner: a plan for organizations with several events (2 of 2)","url":"/partner","text":"The page shows how many of the 10 are used. The badge only shows that the organization has the plan; it is not an endorsement by SoCal Lights. When the organizer's email is at the organization's own website the page also says Verified email domain. Founding city partners have it free through the end of 2026 and see no price. The price is on the Pricing page. Refunds follow the same rules as other paid placements; a refund or chargeback switches off the pins the plan applied."},{"title":"Weekly lights email: sign up and stop","url":"/tutorials#email","text":"The weekly lights email is free: one short email a week with lights and events near a city you choose. To sign up: tap the three dots at the top of the map, beside the search box, and choose Weekly lights email; enter your email and your city, choose how far from the city to look (5, 10 or 25 miles), and tick the box to agree; tap Sign up. We email you a confirmation link and nothing is sent until you tap it, which protects you from anyone signing you up by mistake. You can also sign up from a city page or an event page, where a small box asks only for your email. Every email ends with a one-tap link that deletes your address from the list right away. Contest emails have their own unsubscribe link, which does not stop the weekly email, and the other way round.","kw":"unsubscribe stop emails opt out weekly email newsletter"},{"title":"Is there an app in the App Store or Google Play? (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#app","text":"No, not yet. SoCal Lights is not in the Apple App Store, Google Play or any other app store right now, and there is nothing to search for or download from a store. For now it is a website that works on any phone at socallights.app. You can add it to your phone's Home Screen so it opens full screen with its own icon, like an app. On an iPhone, open socallights.app in Safari, tap the Share button, then Add to Home Screen, then Add. On an Android phone, open it in Chrome, tap the three dots, then Add to Home screen (Chrome may call it Install app; either way it adds the website, not a store app). With no signal it still opens and shows the last list of places it saved on your phone, and it updates by itself when your signal returns."},{"title":"Is there an app in the App Store or Google Play? (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#app","text":"Say \"not yet\" and nothing more: do not promise that a store app is coming or give a date."},{"title":"Refund rules for paid placements and passes","url":"/terms#paid","text":"Featured events, installer placements and contest passes: if we cannot list, feature or apply what you paid for, for any reason, you get a full refund. Once a placement is live, payments are non-refundable, but if your event is cancelled we will move your placement to another of your events or refund the unused time. A pass refunded before its extra contests went live is switched off; if they are live we review it by hand. We make refunds ourselves through Stripe, and a refund or chargeback switches the placement off, so email us before disputing a charge. Nothing limits any refund right you have by law. Questions: info@socallights.app."},{"title":"What an installer can do: all the options for installers (1 of 2)","url":"/pricing#banner","text":"A professional lighting installer has these options, and a free profile comes first. 1) A free company profile page at /installer/<name> with the company, service area, phone, website, a Request a quote button and every home it installed. 2) Add homes you installed: sign in, open Add, choose A lighting display, then A professional installer; your company details are filled in from your profile, and the home counts as confirmed. 3) Homes that homeowners say you installed show Installed by with your company name; you confirm or reject them. 4) Claim and manage your profile: details, logo, areas served, profile views and quote requests. 5) Share listings with homeowners once you confirm them."},{"title":"What an installer can do: all the options for installers (2 of 2)","url":"/pricing#banner","text":"6) Get quote requests from your profile, and from contests that name you as their presenting installer. 7) Optional paid Sponsor placements: the Banner, Top of the list, or both, always labelled Sponsor. Prices are on the Pricing page."},{"title":"Installers: add a home you installed","url":"/add-house","text":"An installer can put a home they worked on on the map. Sign in, open Add (account circle when signed in, three dots when not) and choose A lighting display. At Who's posting, choose A professional installer. Your company name, website, phone and service area are filled in from your profile if you own one. Then give the display a name and a few lines about it, choose the season, add the address, and add night photos. You must tick that you have the homeowner's permission to show the address. Review and tap Put it on the map. A home an installer posts counts as confirmed, appears on your profile with the company name, and joins your profile by company name, so use the same spelling each time. Homeowners can also name your company when they post their own home."},{"title":"Installers: claim and manage your profile","url":"/tutorials#installer-profile","text":"Open your company's profile page and tap Claim this profile at the bottom. If you sign in with an email at your company's website, the profile is yours at once. With any other email, the site owner checks it and emails you. Once it is yours, open your manage page at /installer/<name>/manage. It has your details (website, phone for quotes, areas served, About, logo), the Homes tab (homes that name your company, and homes you share with homeowners), profile views for the last 30 days, the number of homes on the map, your quote requests, and your Sponsor placement with its automatic renewal switch. On the Homes tab, tap Confirm for each home you really installed (it gets a check mark) or Not ours to remove your name from a home you did not install."},{"title":"Installers: homes you share with homeowners","url":"/tutorials#installer-profile","text":"When a homeowner names your company and you confirm, you and the homeowner can both edit that listing: you from the manage page, under Homes you share with homeowners, and the homeowner from their profile. Only the person who created the listing can take it down for the season or put it back. Each change is logged with who changed which field, and both of you are emailed. Homes you posted yourself are edited by anyone who owns your profile. Visitors can find installer displays with the Professionally installed filter, then tap Installed by to open the profile. A check mark means the company confirmed it."},{"title":"Installers: quote requests","url":"/tutorials#presenting-installer","text":"Quote requests reach an installer two ways. On your profile, visitors tap Request a quote; they must be signed in and tick that they agree to share their details. Requests go to your email and are listed on your manage page. A contest organizer can also name you as the contest's presenting installer (Details tab, Presenting installer, with your company, quote email and website); that adds a Get a quote button to the contest page while it is live, and each request goes to you and to the organizer."},{"title":"Installers: Sponsor placements, step by step","url":"/sponsor","text":"Your free profile stays free. Paid Sponsor placements are optional, always labelled Sponsor, and never affect any contest. The Banner is a pill in the strip at the top of the map list; Top of the list puts your company card first in the installer list without the banner; the third plan has both. To buy: open Pricing and choose Installers, or open Promote your event and tap See all pricing; tap Choose on a plan; sign in with your company email; choose your company (it needs a free profile first); check the dates, which start today and end on the last day of the season; and tap Continue to payment, which opens Stripe. Automatic renewal is optional and off unless you tick the box, and you can cancel it any time from your manage page. Prices are on the Pricing page."},{"title":"Seasons and kinds of events on the map (1 of 2)","url":"/","text":"The map shows one season at a time. Tap the season button at the top to switch. The seasons are: New Year's & Lunar New Year; Valentine's Day; Spring Blooms, Easter & Cinco de Mayo; Summer & Fourth of July; Halloween & Día de los Muertos; and Thanksgiving & Christmas Lights. The map opens on the season that is on now. Each season has its own kinds of events. Thanksgiving & Christmas Lights: Turkey trots & fun runs, Thanksgiving feasts & harvest, Walk-through lights, Drive-by / neighborhood displays, Boat & street parades, Theme parks & zoos, Tree lightings & villages, Tours & rides, and Shows & concerts. Halloween & Día de los Muertos: Haunted attractions, Pumpkin trails & patches, Trick-or-treat & festivals, Día de los Muertos, Shows & immersive theater, and Theme parks & zoos.","kw":"boat parades walk-through displays walk through drive-by seasons halloween christmas types of events categories"},{"title":"Seasons and kinds of events on the map (2 of 2)","url":"/","text":"New Year's & Lunar New Year: New Year's Eve & fireworks, Noon Year's & family events, Rose Parade & parades, Lunar New Year festivals, and Shows & festivals. Valentine's Day: date nights, dances, chocolate and wine tastings, Cupid runs, lights and light shows, shows and concerts, and family events. Spring: flower fields, whale watching, St. Patrick's Day, Easter egg hunts, Cinco de Mayo, and spring festivals and fairs. Summer: Fourth of July fireworks and parades, county fairs, outdoor concerts and movies, summer festivals and art shows, and theme parks and zoos. To find boat parades, choose the Thanksgiving & Christmas Lights season, tap Filters, and under Type choose Boat & street parades. Walk-through lights are displays you walk through, usually ticketed events.","kw":"boat parades walk-through displays walk through drive-by seasons halloween christmas types of events categories"},{"title":"Map filters: what each one does","url":"/tutorials#map","text":"Tap Filters on the map to narrow the list. When: Any date, Tonight, This weekend, or pick a day. Near: pick a city, then how far from it to look (5, 10 or 25 miles). County: choose one or more counties. Cost: All, Free, or Ticketed. Type: the kinds of events for the season, plus Home displays, Professionally installed and Contests. Visiting a home: Drive by, Walk by, Free parking and Stroller friendly. There is also Confirmed dates only, which hides events whose dates are not yet confirmed, and Clear all. The button at the bottom shows how many places match (Show N places). Active filters appear as chips you can tap to remove.","kw":"filters filter free ticketed cost county distance miles tonight weekend type drive walk parking stroller"},{"title":"On any listing: directions, report a problem, remove a home","url":"/help","text":"Open an event or home and tap Directions to open it in your maps app. To plan several stops use Favorites and Route these. If something looks wrong on a listing, tap Report a problem on it, or see the Help Center article Report a problem; you can also email info@socallights.app. If your own home is on the map and you would rather it were not, tap Not your listing? Remove my home on the listing, or use the Remove my home page. Events listed as Expected have dates that are not confirmed yet; Confirmed means a web page checked the name and date.","kw":"directions get directions report wrong problem bug mistake remove home confirmed expected"},{"title":"Day and night mode","url":"/","text":"Every page has a sun and moon button, in the header on the map and on other pages, that switches between day (light) and night (dark) mode. The site picks one for you at first, and remembers your choice on your device."},{"title":"Your profile: drafts, displays, events, contests, badges and account (1 of 2)","url":"/profile","text":"Open your profile from the account circle (Your profile). It has these tabs. Drafts: add forms keep your place when you tap Save & exit, and here you can Continue or Delete a draft. Contests: the contests you run (Manage or View page) and the homes you entered. Displays: homes you posted, each showing On the map, In review or Taken down for the season, with Edit, Take down for the season (or Put back on the map) and Delete; installers also see Manage the company profile. Events: events you added, with Edit, a notice you can set to Cancelled or Postponed, Run again next year, and Delete. Badges: winners keep a crown badge, and you choose where badges show (list card, map pop-up, details page).","kw":"draft drafts badges delete event edit display edit event profile tabs save exit"},{"title":"Your profile: drafts, displays, events, contests, badges and account (2 of 2)","url":"/profile","text":"Account: your optional name and address (used only to fill in forms, never shown publicly), your monthly update choice, Sign out, and Delete my account. To delete an event or a home, use Delete on its row in your profile.","kw":"draft drafts badges delete event edit display edit event profile tabs save exit"},{"title":"Signing in and out, and passwords","url":"/profile","text":"There are no passwords. Sign in with Google or with a 6-digit code emailed to you. To sign out, tap the account circle and choose Sign out, or open Your profile, Account tab, and tap Sign out. The sign-in lasts up to 30 days on your device unless you sign out. If the emailed code does not arrive, check spam, make sure the address is right, and ask for a new one; codes last 10 minutes.","kw":"sign out log out logout passwords password sign in code email"},{"title":"How payments work: Stripe, receipts and tips","url":"/pricing","text":"Paid options (Featured events, installer Sponsor placements, contest passes, featuring a winning home) are paid on Stripe's secure page, with a card or Cash App Pay. We never see your card details. Stripe emails your receipt, and SoCal Lights sends a short confirmation. There are no subscriptions unless an installer ticks the optional automatic renewal box. If a payment was taken but a placement or pass has not appeared, wait a few minutes and refresh, then email info@socallights.app. Tips: tap the three dots, choose Support SoCal Lights, and use the tip link there. We do not list other payment methods.","kw":"pay payment secure receipt tip tips donate apple pay cash app card stripe"},{"title":"Cancel automatic renewal for an installer Sponsor placement","url":"/sponsor","text":"Automatic renewal is optional and off unless you ticked the box when you bought. To stop it: open the cancel link in any renewal email, or open your installer manage page (/installer/<name>/manage), find Sponsor placement, and tap Turn off automatic renewal. You can turn it on again there. Cancelling stops the next renewal; the season you already paid for keeps running.","kw":"cancel renewal automatic renew stop subscription"},{"title":"Advertising and promoting on SoCal Lights","url":"/pricing","text":"Event organizers can promote an event with a Featured pin, banner or top of the list (Promote your event). Lighting installers can buy a Sponsor Banner or Top of the list placement (Pricing, Installers). Contest organizers can record sponsors on their contest page. All paid placements are labelled and never affect a contest result. Prices are on the Pricing page. For anything else, email info@socallights.app.","kw":"advertise advertising promote cost price list banner buy"},{"title":"Contest organizer tools: QR poster, yard signs, results download","url":"/help#run","text":"On the organizer dashboard you can copy the contest link and print a QR poster that opens the contest page, print entry posters, and print a yard sign. In the print window turn on background graphics so colors print. After winners are announced, the dashboard has Download results (spreadsheet). You can also hide or show vote counts, and read contest activity.","kw":"print qr poster yard sign download results spreadsheet"},{"title":"The Shop tab and affiliate links","url":"/","text":"The map has a Shop tab next to Places and Favorites, with ideas for each season. Its cards open an Amazon search. As an Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying purchases, at no extra cost to you. Shop links never affect contests or listings."},{"title":"Privacy basics: cookies, location, data and the AI assistant","url":"/privacy","text":"We do not sell your personal information and we do not use advertising cookies or ad networks. A sign-in cookie keeps you signed in on your device for up to 30 days, and signing out removes it. Your location is used only on your own device, to show distances and directions; you can say no at any time. Page views are counted as one number per page per day, with no IP address, cookie or account. The site remembers a few choices on your device only, such as your saved places in Favorites, your day or night choice, your maps app and whether you hid the tips. The AI assistant keeps your chat only in your browser tab, and the server stores counts, never your messages. Full details are on the Privacy page.","kw":"cookies cookie location stored data collect sell tracking ads advertising privacy"},{"title":"Is SoCal Lights free? What costs money?","url":"/pricing","text":"Using SoCal Lights is free: the map, search, routes, Favorites, voting, entering a contest, and posting an event or your own home. Residents and visitors use it for free, and organizers pay for a contest pass to run a contest. Optional paid extras are always labelled and never change a contest result: Featured placements for events, installer Sponsor placements (the Banner, Top of the list, or both), contest passes, and featuring a winning home. Prices are on the Pricing page. The site is in English.","kw":"free cost how much price list listing site free pay"},{"title":"Installer and Featured plans: what the Banner includes","url":"/pricing","text":"For events, every Featured plan includes the gold-ringed Featured pin and badge. The banner adds your event as a Featured pill in the strip at the top of the map list, and Top of the list shows your event first when people browse your area; the plans combine these. For installers, the Sponsor Banner is a pill labelled Sponsor in that strip, Top of the list puts your company card first in the installer list without the banner, and the third plan has both. Each plan card on the Pricing page folds open with What's included and the exact price.","kw":"banner plan include includes top of the list pin what do i get"},{"title":"Adding a logo, photos and sponsor logos","url":"/help#sponsors","text":"An installer adds a company logo on the manage page under Your details. A contest organizer adds a sponsor's logo on the Sponsors tab when recording a sponsor, or the sponsor adds it on the private invite link. Logos show only after the organizer confirms the sponsor and chooses to show it. Photos for homes and events are added in the add form (take them at night, the first is the cover), and can be edited from your profile.","kw":"logo logos add a logo upload sponsor logo installer logo photo"},{"title":"Photos: formats, size and tips","url":"/help/my-photo-will-not-upload","text":"You can add up to 8 photos to a home. Photos must be JPG, PNG or WebP, and each one must be under 6 MB. You do not need to resize them: the form shrinks big phone photos for you before they upload, so a normal phone photo works. Take photos at night with the lights on; the first photo is the cover people see on the map. Homes can also add photos later by email. If a photo will not upload, check that it is a JPG, PNG or WebP and under 6 MB, then try again, and see the Help Center article \"My photo will not upload\".","kw":"photo photos picture size how big resolution format jpg png webp upload limit megabytes"},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find lights near me (1 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#map","text":"Find lights near me. Use your location, the seasons, city search and filters to find what is on near you. In this video, you will learn how to find holiday lights and events near you. We will cover the season buttons, searching by city, getting driving routes, and narrowing the list with filters. We start on the map. To begin, open your web browser, and go to SoCal Lights dot app. That is the only address you need. This is the SoCal Lights home page. The first time you visit, it asks to use your location. Tap Use my location. That is how the map shows how far things are, and gives you directions. Your browser asks permission as well, so tap Allow. Your location is used only on your device, and you can say no at any time. The map zooms to where you are."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find lights near me (2 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#map","text":"There are no events on your street, so tap Show all of Southern California. Now every place shows how far away it is, nearest first, so you can plan your drive. The map shows different things at different times of the year, so first choose a season. Tap the season button at the top. It shows what is on right now. Pick the one you want. Christmas lights are the biggest, so tap Thanksgiving and Christmas Lights. Halloween, New Year, and other seasons are in the same list. You can also search for a city. Type its name, and tap it in the list. The map jumps there, which is handy if you are planning a trip to a different part of Southern California. To drive to what you see, tap Route these, the button on the map. On a computer it sits in the top bar, and on a phone, just above the list."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find lights near me (3 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#map","text":"It puts the places in view into driving order. It works for up to forty five places at a time, so zoom in if there are more. Your maps app can only take about nine stops at a time, so a long list is split into routes of nine or fewer. Each route starts where the last one ended, so you do not have to plan the break. Each route opens in Apple Maps, Google Maps, or Waze. On a phone, the site asks which one the first time, and remembers your answer. And you can tick off places as you go, so you can pick up where you left off. Tap the X to close. To narrow the list, tap Filters. You can pick a date, like tonight or this weekend, a city and how far from it to look, a county, free or ticketed, a type, such as home displays, and whether you can walk by a home."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find lights near me (4 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#map","text":"Use it when the list is too long to scan. That is how you find lights near you. Allow your location, so distances and directions work. Pick a season. Search a city. And narrow the list with Filters. In the next video, we will plan a whole night. Next up: Plan a night."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Plan a night (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#night","text":"Plan a night. Save favorites, build a route, share it and add it to your calendar. In this video, you will plan a whole evening of lights. You will save the places you like, put them in a sensible driving order, and send the route to your maps app. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Let's plan a night. First, choose what you want to see. Tap the season button. Pick the one you want. Christmas lights are the biggest, so tap Thanksgiving and Christmas Lights. Halloween, New Year, and other seasons are in the same list. When you see a place you want to visit, tap the heart on its card. That saves it to your favorites. Add a few. I will add three. Now tap the heart tab, Favorites. Your hearts are collected here."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Plan a night (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#night","text":"The stops are put in a sensible driving order, with the miles and the time they add up to, so you do not zigzag across town. You can choose where to start, and which maps app to use. When you are ready to go, tap Route these. Your maps app takes about nine stops at a time, so a long night is split into routes of nine or fewer. Each route starts where the last one ended. A route with nine stops or fewer is a single route. As you go, tick each stop when you have been there. That way, if you stop for dinner, or save the rest for another night, you can see exactly where you left off. Each route has its own button, and it opens in your maps app, ready for turn by turn directions. You can choose Apple Maps, Google Maps, or Waze, and the change button next to Opens in switches between them."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Plan a night (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#night","text":"On a phone, the site asks the first time, and remembers your choice. Waze takes one stop at a time, so it starts with the first stop. Phones also show a Send to car button on each stop. Then tap the X to close. Planning with family or friends? Tap Share to send your favorites to them. They can open the same list on their own phone, and add it to their own favorites. Tap the heart on places you like. Route these splits your night into routes of nine or fewer, each starting where the last one ended. Tick off stops as you go. Next up: Weekly lights email."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Weekly lights email (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#email","text":"Weekly lights email. Sign up for the weekly email and unsubscribe any time. In this video, you will sign up for the weekly lights email, one short email a week with the lights and events near a city you choose. It is free, and you can stop it any time. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See the three dots? That is the menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap them. In the menu, tap Weekly lights email. Type your email address, then your city. We use the city to choose which lights and events to send you. You can also choose how far from the city to look: five, ten, or twenty five miles. Then tick the box to agree to get the email."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Weekly lights email (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#email","text":"It is a free email, once a week, and nothing else. Tap Sign up. We send you an email to confirm. Nothing is sent until you do, and that protects you from anyone signing you up by mistake. Open it, and tap the link. That is it. You will get one email a week with the lights and events near you. And every email ends with a link to stop. One tap, and you are off the list, no questions asked. Next up: Install the app and use it offline."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Install the app and use it offline (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#app","text":"Install the app and use it offline. Add SoCal Lights to your phone and use it without a connection. In this video, you will put SoCal Lights on your phone like an app, and see what still works when you have no signal, which happens a lot when you are driving around looking at lights. On your phone, open your web browser, and go to SoCal Lights dot app. This works on any phone. This is the home page. There is nothing to download from an app store. You add the site to your Home Screen, and it opens like any other app, full screen, with its own icon. On an iPhone, use Safari. Tap the Share button, the square with an arrow. Scroll down, and tap Add to Home Screen. Then tap Add. On an Android phone, use Chrome. Tap the three dots in Chrome itself, at the top of the browser, not on the site."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Install the app and use it offline (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#app","text":"Then tap Install app, or Add to Home screen. Chrome offers this because SoCal Lights is a website, not an app from a store. Now the useful part. With no signal, the app still opens, because the site saves your last list of places on your phone. So when you are out looking at lights, in a part of town with weak coverage, you can still see what you were looking at. When your signal comes back, the app updates by itself, with the latest events. You do not have to do anything. Next up: Find contests."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find contests: the Contests page (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#contests","text":"Find contests: the Contests page. Live now, Mine and Past results, and the Enter or vote button on a map card. In this video, you will find the neighborhood contests, where you can vote for your favorite homes, enter your own, or see who won. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. This opens on your own page, Mine. To see every contest that is open today, tap Live now. Live now lists the contests that are open today."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find contests: the Contests page (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#contests","text":"On each one, you can vote for your favorite homes, or enter your own home. Voting and entering each have their own dates, shown on the card, so a contest shows what you can do right now. Tap Mine. This is your personal page. It lists the contests you run, if you run any, with buttons to manage them or view them, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Past results, for contests that are finished. You can see the winners in every category, and look back at earlier years. That is the Contests page: Live now, Mine, and Past results. Contests also show up on the map as cards, with a link to enter your home or vote, so you may find one without ever opening this page. If you are not signed in, the same page is under the three dots, then Contests."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find contests: the Contests page (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#contests","text":"Next up: Find professionally installed homes."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find professionally installed homes (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#pro-filter","text":"Find professionally installed homes. Use the Professionally installed filter, then open an installer's profile. In this video, you will find homes that a professional lighting company installed, and look at the company behind them. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Tap the season button. Pick the one you want. Christmas lights are the biggest, so tap Thanksgiving and Christmas Lights. Halloween, New Year, and other seasons are in the same list. Tap Filters. Under Type, tap Professionally installed. This shows only homes that a lighting company put up, which is great if you are thinking of hiring one for your own home. Tap the button at the bottom, Show places, with the number of places it found."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Find professionally installed homes (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#pro-filter","text":"Open one of the places. It says Installed by, with the company's name. A check mark means the company has confirmed it. Tap the company's name to open its profile. This is the company's profile. It lists where they work, their phone number, and their website, along with every home they have installed, so you can see their work before you call. Next up: Enter your home in a contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Enter your home in a contest (1 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#enter","text":"Enter your home in a contest. Sign in, add photos, your address and agree to the rules. In this video, you will enter your home in a neighborhood contest. It is a short step by step form: photos, your address, and a name, and then you agree to the rules. It takes just a few minutes. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. This opens on your own page, Mine. To see every contest that is open today, tap Live now."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Enter your home in a contest (2 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#enter","text":"Under Live now, find the contest, and tap Enter my home. If you are not signed in yet, the form starts with a sign in step, with Google, or with your email. That keeps people from entering on someone else's behalf. This video picks up after you sign in. The form takes you one screen at a time. First, photos. Show the lights at their best, so take them at night with everything on. Tap Choose photos. You can pick up to eight, and the first one becomes the cover photo that people see first. Tap Next. Next, your address. The city is already set to the contest's city. If your home is somewhere else, there is a link to change it. Type your street address and ZIP code."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Enter your home in a contest (3 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#enter","text":"This is where the pin goes on the map, and visitors will see it so they can drive by, so only enter an address you are happy to share. Now the name to show. This is how your entry appears to voters, for example, The Kim family. You can add a line about your home, like, the roof is synchronized to music. It is optional, but it helps neighbors choose. The last screen lists what you entered. Each line has an Edit link, if you spot a mistake. Tick the box to agree to the official rules, which you can read first. Then tap Submit my entry. You are entered! Organizers choose whether entries go on the map right away, or whether they review each one first. In this contest, the organizer reviews each entry, so it may take a little while to appear, and you will get an email when it is approved."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Enter your home in a contest (4 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#enter","text":"You can enter more than one home, up to five in each contest, with Enter another home. And you can come back to edit your entry, which we cover next. Next up: Edit or withdraw your entry."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Edit or withdraw your entry (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#edit-entry","text":"Edit or withdraw your entry. Change your entry's name and description, or take it out. In this video, you will change the name and description of your entry, and then see how to take your home out of a contest, if you ever need to. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. Under Homes you entered, you will find your entry, and the contest it is in. Tap Open contest. Tap Open contest. Find the card called Your home."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Edit or withdraw your entry (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#edit-entry","text":"This is where you manage your entry. Tap Edit name and description. Change the name, or add a better description, then tap Save. You can do this any time while the contest is open. If you ever need to take your home out of the contest, tap Withdraw. It asks you to tap again, so you cannot do it by accident. Once you do, your home leaves the contest and the map. Next up: Your profile."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Your profile (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#profile","text":"Your profile. Drafts, contests, badges and deleting your account. In this video, you will tour your profile: your saved drafts, your contests, the badges your home has won, and your account settings. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Your profile is two taps from the map. Look at the top of the map, and tap your picture, or your initial, in the circle. In the menu, tap Your profile. This is your profile. At the top is your picture, or your initial, with a button to add or change your photo. Below it, Drafts lists anything you started and saved, like an event or a contest you did not finish. Because it is saved to your account, you can finish it later, on any device. Tap Contests."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Your profile (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#profile","text":"This lists the contests you run, with buttons to manage each one or view its page, and below that, the homes you entered in other people's contests, with where each one stands. Displays and Events, the next two tabs, list the homes and events you posted. Tap Badges. When your home wins a contest, it earns a badge, and a crown on the map. All the awards your home has won are listed here, from every year. Tap Account. This is where you change your name, city, and address. They are optional, they fill in forms for you, and they are never shown publicly. You can also choose the monthly update email, sign out, and, at the bottom, delete your account, along with everything tied to it, if you ever want to. That is your profile: your drafts, your contests, your badges, and your account."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Your profile (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#profile","text":"If you ever need help, there is a Help Center in the menu, with written steps for everything in these videos. Next up: Vote in a contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Vote in a contest (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#vote","text":"Vote in a contest. Use the category tabs, pick one favorite in each, and share the contest. In this video, you will vote for your favorite homes in a neighborhood contest, and see how voting works, so you know your vote counts. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. This opens on your own page, Mine. To see every contest that is open today, tap Live now. Under Live now, find the contest, and tap Vote."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Vote in a contest (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#vote","text":"The first time, the site asks you to sign in. That keeps it fair, with one vote per person in each category. Want to see the homes in person before you decide? Tap Visit the homes. It splits the homes into routes of nine stops or fewer, and sends each one to your maps app. Each route starts where the last one ended, and you can tick off each home as you go. Now the ballot. Every home is in every category, and you pick one favorite in each. Start with the first category, Best Traditional. Look through the homes, and tap Vote on your favorite. The next category opens by itself. In Most Creative, tap Vote on the home you think is the most inventive. And last, Best Animated. Tap your favorite. Thanks for voting! Your votes are saved, and you can change any of them until voting closes."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Vote in a contest (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#vote","text":"You cannot vote for your own home. And tap Share this contest, to send it to a neighbor, because more votes make the contest more fun. Next up: Judge a contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Judge a contest (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#judge","text":"Judge a contest. Open your judge link, score each home, and recuse yourself when you know the household. In this video, you will judge a contest. You will open your invitation email, see how your ballot works, and pick or score the homes, including what to do when you know the household. This is your invitation email. It says which contest it is, who invited you, and how judging works. The organizer chooses one of two methods. Either you pick a favorite home in each category, or you score every home from one to ten. This contest uses scores. Everything you need is behind one button. Tap Open my judges' ballot. This is your ballot. It says when voting closes, and how many are done, so you always know how far along you are."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Judge a contest (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#judge","text":"Your picks or scores stay private, and you can change them until voting closes. Before you score, you may want to see the homes in person. Tap Visit the homes. A long list is split into routes of nine stops or fewer, and each one opens in your maps app. Each route starts where the last one ended, and you tick off each home as you go. Now tap a category. Every home is in every category. If the organizer chose to pick a favorite, each home has a Pick button, and after you pick, the others say Move my pick. In this contest, you score each home once for each award. Do you know the household, perhaps a friend or a relative? Tap I know this household. That home is skipped for you, so your score cannot be questioned later. Score each of the other homes from one to ten."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Judge a contest (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#judge","text":"If your contest uses picks, you would tap Pick on your favorite instead. Judge on first impression, creativity, theme, craftsmanship, and variety of lights. Tap Ballot to see your progress. When you have scored every home, you are done. Your scores stay private until the winners are announced. Next up: Create a contest with Quick start."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Quick start (1 of 6)","url":"/tutorials#quick","text":"Create a contest with Quick start. The short setup: seven screens. Let's create a neighborhood decorating contest. With Quick start, you will have a live contest, with its own link and QR code, in a few minutes. I will explain what every screen is for as we go, so you always know what you are choosing. We will start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. The map zooms to where you are. There are no events on your street, so tap Show all of Southern California. Now every place shows how far away it is, nearest first, so you can plan your drive. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Quick start (2 of 6)","url":"/tutorials#quick","text":"In the menu, tap Add an event, home or contest. That is where you add anything new: an event, a contest, or your own home. This is the Add page. It has three choices. An event, like a tree lighting or a holiday parade. A contest, where neighbors enter their homes, and the community votes for a favorite. Or a lighting display, to show off your own home. We are creating a contest, so tap A contest. First, the name. This is the title everyone sees, on the contest page, on the map, and in the emails your neighbors get. Something simple and local works best, like your street or neighborhood, and the year. I will type Oak Hill Holiday Lights 2026. Then tap Next. Next, who is running it, and where. Type the name of your group."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Quick start (3 of 6)","url":"/tutorials#quick","text":"It can be a city, a school, an H O A, a club, a business, or just your neighbors on one street. Then pick the city the homes are in. That is how your contest shows up for the right neighbors. Tap Next. This is the welcome message. Neighbors read it before they decide to enter, so it is your chance to say what the contest is about. We already wrote a friendly one for you. You can keep it as it is, or change a few words so it sounds like you. Tap Next. A website is optional. If your group has one, add it here, and people can see who is behind the contest. Your contest goes live right away either way, so it is fine to skip this. Tap Next. Now the dates. There are three. The last day neighbors can enter. The day voting starts. And the day voting ends."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Quick start (4 of 6)","url":"/tutorials#quick","text":"Entries and voting open and close on these dates by themselves. We filled in typical dates, so you can just tap Next. You can change any of them later, from your dashboard. This is the big choice, how the rules are set up. Quick start is the recommended one, and it is what we are using. It turns on public voting, where anyone with an account gets one vote in each category. It gives you four voting categories: Best Overall, Most Creative, Best Lights and Music, and Best Traditional. It lets you approve every entry before it reaches the map. And it writes your official rules for you. Everything can still be changed later. If you would rather choose judges, prizes, or your own categories, pick Customize everything. We cover that in the next video. For now, choose Quick start, then tap Next."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Quick start (5 of 6)","url":"/tutorials#quick","text":"The last screen is the review. It lists everything you chose: the name, who runs it, the dates, the categories, and who can vote. If you run more, you choose a pass first: see the Pricing page. If something is not right, tap Edit next to it. Nothing is public until you tap Create contest. When you are happy, tap Create contest. That is it. Your contest is live. This page explains what happens next. You share your link or your QR code. Neighbors enter their homes. You look over each entry before it goes on the map. Then the community votes, on the dates you set, and you announce the winners. Tap Open my dashboard. This is your dashboard. It is where you run the contest. Up here are the numbers that matter: your entries, the ones waiting for you, and the votes."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Quick start (6 of 6)","url":"/tutorials#quick","text":"And here is your link, and your QR code, ready to paste into a message, or print for a flyer. We will cover sharing in a moment. Next up: Create a contest with Customize."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (1 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"Create a contest with Customize. Judges, prizes, categories, vote counts and your own rules. In this video, you will set up a contest exactly your way: your own voting categories, invited judges, a prize, and your own rules. I will explain every choice as we go, so you know what each one does. We will start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. The map zooms to where you are. There are no events on your street, so tap Show all of Southern California. Now every place shows how far away it is, nearest first, so you can plan your drive. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (2 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"In the menu, tap Add an event, home or contest. That is where you add anything new: an event, a contest, or your own home. This is the Add page, with three choices. We want a contest. We are creating a contest, so tap A contest. I will type Pine Ridge Holiday Lights 2026. Then tap Next. Next, who is running it. Type the name of your group. Then pick the city the homes are in, and tap Next. The welcome message is already written for you. Keep it, or change it to sound like you. Tap Next. A website is optional, and your contest goes live right away without one. Tap Next. These three dates are filled in for you: the last day to enter, and when voting starts and ends. Tap Next. Here is the big choice. Quick start sets everything up for you."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (3 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"Customize everything lets you decide each rule yourself. We want to decide, so choose Customize everything, then tap Next. Now the voting categories. Every home competes in every category, and each voter picks one favorite home in each. So the categories are the awards you will give out. These four are suggested. Tap one to turn it off. Best Overall is a category like any other, so you can keep it, or drop it. You need at least one. You can add your own. Type a name, like Best Inflatable, and tap Add. Funny, local categories are a big part of the fun. Then tap Next. Next, who gets to vote. There are three choices. Everyone means anyone with an account, one vote per category. That award is called People's Choice. Judges only means just the people you invite can vote."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (4 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"That award is called Judges' Choice. And Everyone and judges does both. The public and your judges vote separately, so every category has two winners, one chosen by the neighborhood and one by the judges. Judges do not vote in the public vote. We will choose that one, then tap Next. Now your judges. Type one email address on each line. Each judge gets an invitation with a private link, and signs in with that same email. Choose people your neighbors will trust, like a city staff member, a teacher, or a local lighting company. I will add two. You can add or remove judges later from your dashboard, so nothing is final. Tap Next. How do judges vote? Pick a favorite is simple and quick. Each judge chooses one home in each category, the same way the public does."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (5 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"Score every home is for formal judging. Each judge gives every home a score from one to ten, in every category, and the highest average wins. With five or more judges, the highest and lowest score for each home are dropped, so one unusual judge cannot decide the result. We will choose score, then tap Next. How should new entries be approved? I review each entry first is the recommended choice. Nothing appears on the map until you approve it, so you can screen out photos that do not belong. Publish entries right away is faster, but you cannot screen photos first. We will keep the recommended choice. Tap Next. Should people see the vote counts while voting is open? Hiding them until you announce winners is recommended. It stops bandwagon voting, where people just vote for whoever is ahead."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (6 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"Showing running totals builds excitement, but everyone can see who is ahead. You always see the counts yourself on your dashboard. Keep the recommended choice and tap Next. A few fairness rules. The first is whether one home can win more than one category. By default it can, because the best home in each category simply wins. One win per home spreads the awards around, so a home that wins one category cannot win a later one. Below, you can see what is already protected for you: one vote per person per category, tied to a verified email, nobody can vote for their own home, and ties are shared. Keep the default and tap Next. Is there a prize? This is optional. A prize is shown on your contest page, and with the winners when you announce them."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (7 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"No prize still gives winners a crown and recognition. You can give the same prize to every winner, or a different prize for each award. We will choose one prize for all winners. Describe it, for example, a fifty dollar gift card. If a local business is providing it, you can name them here too. Then tap Next. These are your official rules. We drafted them for you, and people have to accept them before they enter. They cover who can enter, how winners are chosen, and what the prize terms are. You can edit anything. If you offer a prize, it is a good idea to have a lawyer read them. Tap Next. Last is the review. Everything you chose is listed here, and each line has an Edit link if you want to change it. If you run more, you choose a pass first: see the Pricing page."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Create a contest with Customize (8 of 8)","url":"/tutorials#custom","text":"Nothing is public until you tap Create contest. When you are happy with it, tap Create contest. Your contest is live. This page explains what happens next. Share your link, neighbors enter, you approve the entries, the community and your judges vote, and you announce the winners. Tap Open my dashboard. This is your dashboard, where you run the contest, with your link and QR code ready to share. Sharing is the next video. Next up: Share your contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Contest passes (1 of 5)","url":"/tutorials#contest-passes","text":"Contest passes. What the pass picker shows when you run a contest, and how to choose a pass. In this video, you will see what happens when you run a second contest in the same year. After that, you choose a pass. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. The map zooms to where you are. There are no events on your street, so tap Show all of Southern California. Now every place shows how far away it is, nearest first, so you can plan your drive. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Add an event, home or contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Contest passes (2 of 5)","url":"/tutorials#contest-passes","text":"That is where you add anything new: an event, a contest, or your own home. This is the Add page. It has three choices. An event, like a tree lighting or a holiday parade. A contest, where neighbors enter their homes, and the community votes for a favorite. Or a lighting display, to show off your own home. We are creating a contest, so tap A contest. First, the name. This is the title everyone sees, on the contest page, on the map, and in the emails your neighbors get. Something simple and local works best, like your street or neighborhood, and the year. I will type Oak Hill Holiday Lights 2027. Then tap Next. Next, who is running it, and where. Type the name of your group. It can be a city, a school, an H O A, a club, a business, or just your neighbors on one street."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Contest passes (3 of 5)","url":"/tutorials#contest-passes","text":"Then pick the city the homes are in. That is how your contest shows up for the right neighbors. Tap Next. This is the welcome message. Neighbors read it before they decide to enter, so it is your chance to say what the contest is about. We already wrote a friendly one for you. You can keep it as it is, or change a few words so it sounds like you. Tap Next. A website is optional. If your group has one, add it here, and people can see who is behind the contest. Your contest goes live right away either way, so it is fine to skip this. Tap Next. Now the dates. There are three. The last day neighbors can enter. The day voting starts. And the day voting ends. Entries and voting open and close on these dates by themselves. We filled in typical dates, so you can just tap Next."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Contest passes (4 of 5)","url":"/tutorials#contest-passes","text":"You can change any of them later, from your dashboard. Choose Quick start, then tap Next. This is the review. Your organization already ran a contest in the last 12 months, so this one needs a pass. Tap Create contest. Instead of creating the contest, the page shows the passes. It says where your organization stands in its 12-month window, and how many contests you have used. Each card lists everything it gives you, and the price is on the card. Running more than twenty contests? The Up to 20 card tells you what to do. You write to us, and we set it up with you. To go on, tap Choose under the pass you want. That opens Stripe's secure payment page. We stop here, because this is only a demonstration, and we never enter card details in a video."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Contest passes (5 of 5)","url":"/tutorials#contest-passes","text":"After you pay, your contest is created by itself, with everything you typed. If you need more contests later, choose a bigger pass, and you pay only the difference. Entering and voting stay free for everyone, and payment never affects who wins. Next up: Create a contest with Quick start."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Share your contest (1 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#share","text":"Share your contest. Link, QR code, entry poster and yard sign. In this video, you will learn every way to share your contest: the link, the QR code, a printable entry poster and yard sign, and an email invitation to your neighbors. The more people who know about it, the better the contest. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Share your contest (2 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#share","text":"It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Manage, next to your contest. This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. This is the Overview, the first page of your dashboard. Scroll down to the card called Share it. Everything you need to get the word out is in this one card. At the top is your contest link. Tap Copy link, and it is copied for you. Paste it into an email, a text message, a neighborhood group, or a Facebook post. Anyone who opens it lands on your contest page, where they can see the homes, enter their own, and vote. Below the link are your two ready-made posters, with your contest name, the dates, and a QR code already on them."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Share your contest (3 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#share","text":"When someone points their phone camera at the QR code, it opens your contest page. That makes them perfect for anything printed, like flyers, signs, and the bulletin board at your local coffee shop. Tap Print entry poster for the one made for a bulletin board or a shop window. Here is a sample. It is festive, so it gets noticed, and it shows your contest name, a big QR code, the deadline to enter, and the words, scan to enter your home. Its job is to get neighbors to enter. When you print, turn on Background graphics in the print window, so the colors show. Print yard sign makes a big sign for a lawn. Here is a sample. It says vote in huge letters, so people can read it from the street, with a QR code that opens the voting page, and the voting dates."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Share your contest (4 of 4)","url":"/tutorials#share","text":"Put these out when voting opens, and your neighbors can vote while they are standing in front of the lights. You can also invite neighbors by email, right from the dashboard. Want to know whether your sharing is working? Tap Promote. Open Invite neighbors by email. Type one address on each line, and add a short personal note. A note from a real neighbor gets many more people to open the invitation than a plain message does. This is what each neighbor receives. It has your contest name, your note, and one clear button that takes them straight to the contest. They do not need to hunt for anything. Next up: Approve and decline entries."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Approve and decline entries (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#approve","text":"Approve and decline entries. Approve, decline with a reason, add homes yourself, and undo a decline. In this video, you will review the homes your neighbors enter, add homes yourself, and undo a mistake. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine, your personal page. Tap Manage, next to your contest. Now tap Entries. Nothing goes on the map until you approve it. At the top is Add homes yourself, for homes you already know about."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Approve and decline entries (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#approve","text":"Tap it to open. Paste one address per line, with a name after a bar, if you like. Then tap Check addresses. Nothing is added yet. Each line is looked up. Found means ready. Not found means a typo. Already in means it is in your contest. Tick the box to confirm the residents agreed to have their address shown. Then tap Add 3 homes. The new homes are on the map and the ballot, tagged Added by organizer. Added one by mistake? Tap Remove, then tap again to confirm. Tap again. Homes your neighbors enter wait for you. If one looks right, tap Approve. It joins the contest, and the neighbor is emailed. If one does not belong, tap Decline. A kind note helps the neighbor try again. Then tap Decline entry. Changed your mind? Tap Undo decline. Next up: Add your city's homes yourself."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add your city's homes yourself (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#add-homes","text":"Add your city's homes yourself. Paste a list of addresses, check them, and put every home on the ballot. In this video, you will add a whole list of homes to your contest yourself, for example when your city already collects registrations somewhere else. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine, your personal page. Tap Manage, next to your contest. Now tap Entries. Homes can be added here until voting starts."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add your city's homes yourself (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#add-homes","text":"Open Add homes yourself. Each home goes straight onto the ballot and the routes, with no photo, and no sign in. Paste one home per line: the street address, city and ZIP, then a bar, and the family name. The city, ZIP and name are optional. Paste your list, up to a hundred at a time. Tap Check addresses. This looks up each address, and adds nothing yet. Found means ready. Not found means a typo, so fix it and check again. Already in means it is in your contest. Tick the box to confirm that the residents agreed to have their address shown on the public map. The button now says how many homes it will add. Tap Add 3 homes. The three homes that were found are on the map and the ballot, tagged Added by organizer. The two that were not found stay in the box, so you can fix them."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add your city's homes yourself (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#add-homes","text":"Added one by mistake? Tap Remove. Then tap again to confirm. That is how you put a whole neighborhood on the ballot. Homes can be added until voting starts. Next up: Edit your contest and change the dates."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Edit your contest and change the dates (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#edit","text":"Edit your contest and change the dates. Change the details any time before winners are announced. In this video, you will change your contest after you have created it: the dates, the name, the message, the prize, and the rules. Plans change, and the site is built for that. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Edit your contest and change the dates (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#edit","text":"It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Manage, next to your contest. This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. Plans change, and you can change your contest at any time before you announce winners. On the Overview, find Schedule, which shows when entries close and when voting runs. Tap Change dates. That opens the Details tab, where you edit your contest. Here you can move the day entries close, the day voting starts, and the day voting ends. Voting should start after entries close, and end after it starts. If you need more time for neighbors to enter, move these dates later. Scroll down and you can also change the contest name, the welcome message, the prize, and the official rules."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Edit your contest and change the dates (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#edit","text":"Neighbors see the changes right away. When you are done, tap Save changes. Everyone on your team is emailed about the change, so nobody is surprised, and you have a record of what changed. Only the person who created the contest can make these changes. One more card is on this tab: Run it again. Next year, it starts a new contest with the same rules, categories, prizes, sponsors and judges, and every date a year later. You tap twice to confirm, and you can edit it before it opens. A new contest may need a pass. Next up: Run voting."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Run voting (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#voting","text":"Run voting. Start now, pause, review votes and void a vote. In this video, you will learn how to run voting once it has started. You will pause and resume it, check the results, and remove a vote that should not count. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine, your personal page. Tap Manage, next to your contest. This card is where you control the contest while it is running. Voting opens and closes on your dates by itself."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Run voting (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#voting","text":"The Controls card lets you step in when you need to. The last row, Contest page, has an Unpublish button. It hides the whole contest from the public, and you can publish it again. The next video shows how. Say you spot something odd, or you need to fix a mistake. Tap Pause voting, and votes stop right away, until you say otherwise. When you are ready, tap Resume voting, and voting carries on. Now tap Results. Here is the full tally, category by category. You can always see the counts, even when you have hidden them from the public so people are not swayed by who is ahead. Open Review individual votes. Every single vote is listed, with when it was cast. If a vote should not count, tap Void next to it. It is removed from the tally, and you can restore it later if you change your mind."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Run voting (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#voting","text":"This keeps the contest fair. Next up: Unpublish and publish again."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Unpublish and publish again (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#unpublish","text":"Unpublish and publish again. Hide your contest from the public, then bring it back. In this video, you will hide your contest from the public with two taps, and bring it back when you are ready. Nothing is deleted. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine, your personal page. Tap Manage, next to your contest. On the Overview, the Controls card has a row called Contest page. It says Public and on the map."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Unpublish and publish again (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#unpublish","text":"That means anyone can find the contest. Need to stop everything for a moment, for example to fix a mistake? Tap Unpublish. The button now says, Tap again to unpublish, so it cannot happen by accident. Tap again. Entries and votes stop, and the contest leaves the map. A red notice now sits at the top. It says the contest is hidden, and that nothing has been deleted. Your team can still open this dashboard. When you are ready, tap Publish it again. The notice is gone, the Contest page row says Public and on the map again, and entries and votes carry on, with everything still in place. That is Unpublish: hide the contest, fix what you need to, and publish it again. Nothing is lost. Next up: Add co-organizers and judges."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add co-organizers and judges (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#team","text":"Add co-organizers and judges. Invite people to help run the contest. In this video, you will bring in help. You will add co-organizers, who share the work of running the contest, and judges, who score the homes. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page. It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Manage, next to your contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add co-organizers and judges (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#team","text":"This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. Running a contest is easier with help. Tap Team. Co-organizers sign in and see your dashboard, so they can approve entries, review votes, and manage judges and sponsors. Only you, the creator, can change the contest details, pause voting, unpublish, or announce the winners. Only the creator can add co-organizers, and you can add up to eight. Type their email address, and tap Add co-organizer. They get an email like this one, with one button that opens the dashboard. They sign in with that same email address, and they are in. Judges are different from co-organizers. They score the homes, but they do not manage the contest. They are added on the Judges tab, so tap Judges. Type a judge's email address, and tap Add judge."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add co-organizers and judges (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#team","text":"You can see who has signed in, and how many homes each judge has scored, so you know the judging is on track. Your judge gets an invitation like this one. It says which contest it is, how judging works, and has a button that opens their private ballot. Nothing else to set up. Next up: Announce winners."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Announce winners (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#announce","text":"Announce winners. Close voting, announce, export results and see the archive. In this video, you will finish your contest. You will check the results, close voting, and announce the winners, and see what everyone else sees. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page. It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Manage, next to your contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Announce winners (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#announce","text":"This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. When voting is over, it is time to announce the winners. Tap Results. This shows who would win if you announced today, in every category, so there are no surprises. Tap Download results to save everything to a spreadsheet. It is a good idea to keep a copy for your records. When you are ready, tap Close voting and announce winners. It asks you to tap again, because this cannot be undone. Once you confirm, the results are saved and locked. Everyone who entered is emailed, and so are your judges and you, and the winners get a congratulations message. Voters are not emailed. If voting has not started, nobody has voted, or the judges are not finished, the button first asks, Announce anyway, as a warning."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Announce winners (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#announce","text":"To see what everyone else sees, tap See it as voters do. Here are the winners, with their awards. Winning homes keep a crown on the map and in their owners' profiles, so the recognition lasts all year. Next up: Read your contest activity."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Read your contest activity (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#activity","text":"Read your contest activity. Visits, QR scans, voters and votes per day. In this video, you will see how many people found your contest: page visits, visits from your QR code, and votes, day by day. That tells you which kind of sharing works. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page. It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Read your contest activity (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#activity","text":"Tap Manage, next to your contest. This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. Want to know whether your sharing is working? Tap Promote. Activity shows three numbers: how many people have visited your contest page, how many arrived by scanning your QR code, and how many people have voted. Below, a chart shows the votes for each day. Use it to see which sharing worked best. If votes jump the day after a flyer went up, or after an email, do more of that. Next up: Add sponsors and logos."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Record a sponsor on your contest page (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsors","text":"Record a sponsor on your contest page. Record a sponsor and show it on the contest page. In this video, you will record a local business that paid you to sponsor your contest, so its name and logo are thanked on your contest page. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page. It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Manage, next to your contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Record a sponsor on your contest page (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsors","text":"This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. A sponsor is a local business that supports your contest. This tab is where you record one. Tap Sponsors. Type the company's name. The Level is already set, and you can change it. Then choose their logo, a J P G, P N G, or WebP file under six hundred kilobytes. Now how the sponsorship counts. The sponsor paid you yourself, so choose Paid to me. Tick that you collected the payment, and add the date and how it was paid. Sponsors pay you, never SoCal Lights. Tick that you have the sponsor's permission, and tick Show on the contest page. Tap Save sponsor. Payment never affects who wins. To check how it looks, tap See it as voters do, and you will see the sponsor on the contest page, just as your neighbors do."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Record a sponsor on your contest page (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsors","text":"Next up: Invite a sponsor to your contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Invite a sponsor to your contest (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsor-invite","text":"Invite a sponsor to your contest. Share one private link, then review and approve the request. In this video, you will send a sponsor one private link, then review their request and approve it. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page. It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Manage, next to your contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Invite a sponsor to your contest (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsor-invite","text":"This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. Tap Sponsors. First, tell sponsors how to pay you. Paste your own payment link, or write where to send a check. Tap Save. Next, Invite a sponsor. This works once your contest is live and approved. Tap Create invite link. This is your private link. Tap Copy link, then send it to the sponsor. Now the sponsor's side. They open your link, no account needed, and see how to pay you. They fill in a short form, and tap Send to the organizer. Back on your side, the request waits for you. Nothing shows until you approve it. Once they have paid you, tap Mark payment received. Choose Paid to me, tick that you collected it, add the date, then tap Save sponsor. Now tap Approve. The sponsor appears on your contest page, labelled Sponsor."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Invite a sponsor to your contest (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsor-invite","text":"Next up: Presenting installer and quote requests."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Presenting installer and quote requests (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#installer","text":"Presenting installer and quote requests. How the Get a quote button works. In this video, you will add a lighting company as the presenting installer of your contest, and see how neighbors can ask them for a quote with one tap. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Your contests. That is where the contests you run, and the homes you entered, live. Tap Mine. This is your personal page. It lists the contests you run, if you run any, and the homes you entered, with their status. Tap Manage, next to your contest."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Presenting installer and quote requests (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#installer","text":"This opens your dashboard, the control room for your contest. A lighting company can sponsor your contest as its presenting installer, and neighbors can ask them for a quote. This is set up on the Details tab, so tap Details, and scroll down to Presenting installer. Type the company's name. Then the email address where quote requests should go. That is the address the company checks for new customers, and each request is emailed to you as well. And add their website. Tap Save changes. This adds a Get a quote button to your contest page, so families who love the lights can ask the company for the same thing at their own home. The button shows only while your contest is live. Now let's see it from a neighbor's side. They open the contest page. They need to be signed in to ask."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Presenting installer and quote requests (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#installer","text":"Then they tap Get a quote. They add their name, a phone number if they like, and describe what they would like done. They must tick the box that says they agree to share their details. Without that tick, nothing is sent. Then they tap Send request. The request goes to the company and to you, the contest organizer, and SoCal Lights keeps a copy. Back on your side, open the Promote tab. Every quote request is listed there, with the neighbor's name and details. Next up: Installer profiles and Installed by."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Installer profiles and \"Installed by\" (1 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#installer-profile","text":"Installer profiles and \"Installed by\". How a company gets a profile, how a home links to it, and how to confirm or claim yours. In this video, you will see how a lighting company gets a profile on SoCal Lights, how homes link to it with Installed by, and how a company claims and manages its own profile. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Tap the season button. Tap Thanksgiving and Christmas Lights. Let's look at how a lighting company gets a profile. A homeowner can say which company installed their lights. Search for a home that does. Type its name, and open it. It says Installed by, with the company's name. If there is a check mark next to the name, the company has confirmed that it did the job."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Installer profiles and \"Installed by\" (2 of 2)","url":"/tutorials#installer-profile","text":"Without one, the homeowner says it did, but the company has not confirmed it yet. Tap the company's name to open its profile page. At the bottom, a company can tap Claim this profile. If its email is at its website, the profile is its own at once. Otherwise the site owner checks it, and emails the company. Once the profile is yours, you manage it here, with your company details and logo. Homes that name your company are listed on the Homes tab. Tap Confirm for each home you really installed. It gets a check mark, which tells visitors it is true, and then you and the homeowner can both edit that listing. If it is not yours, tap Not ours, and the name is removed from that home. Next up: Sponsor your installer company."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Sponsor your installer company (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsor","text":"Sponsor your installer company. Banner, Top of the list, or both: pick your company, check the dates, and stop at the secure payment page. In this video, you will sponsor your installer company, so it stands out at the top of the map list, in the installer list, or both. We go as far as the secure payment page, and stop there. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Look at the top right corner of the map, and tap the three dots. In the menu, tap Promote your event. This page is for paid placements. At the top, tap See all pricing. The Pricing page is split by who you are. Tap Installers. Your company profile is free, and it comes first. Below the free profile are three paid placements."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Sponsor your installer company (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsor","text":"Paid placements are always labelled Sponsor. The Banner is a pill in the strip at the top of the map list. Top of the list puts your company first in the installer list. The third card takes both. Each card folds open. Tap What's included, to see everything the Banner gives you. Under Banner, tap Choose. You sign in with your company email, and we already are. Then pick your company. It needs a free profile first. These are the three plans: Banner, Top of the list, and both together. Your choice is marked, and each one lists everything it includes. Below is a summary with the exact dates. It starts today, and ends on the last day of the season. Automatic renewal is optional. It is off unless you tick this box, and you can cancel it any time. Then tap Continue to payment."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Sponsor your installer company (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#sponsor","text":"This opens Stripe's secure page. We stop here, because this is only a demonstration, and we never enter card details in a video. That is how you sponsor your installer company. Every paid placement is labelled Sponsor, and renewal is always your choice. Next up: Add an event to the map."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add an event to the map (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#add-event","text":"Add an event to the map. Share a tree lighting, market, parade or haunted house. In this video, you will add an event to the map, like a tree lighting, a market, a parade, or a haunted house, so families across Southern California can find it. You need to be signed in to add an event, and in this video we already are. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Add an event, home or contest. That is where you add anything new: an event, a contest, or your own home. Now tap An event."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add an event to the map (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#add-event","text":"Events are for things the whole community can attend, like a tree lighting, a holiday parade, a market, or a haunted house. The first question is whether your event has a web page. If it does, paste the address here. When the page mentions your event's name and date, your event is marked Confirmed on the map, which people trust more. If you do not have one, tap No, Next, and the event still goes on the map. Type the event's name, the way people know it. Then choose the season it belongs to, and the type of event. That is how visitors find it when they filter the map. Pick the first and last day. For a one-day event, pick the same day twice. Then tap Next. Is it free to attend, or paid? Families plan around this, so say it clearly. Tap Free, then Next. Now where."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Add an event to the map (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#add-event","text":"Type the name of the place, the city, and the street address. The street address puts a pin in the right spot on the map, so families can get directions. Describe it in a sentence or two. What will families see and do? That is the text people read before they decide to come. Last, check everything. You can change any line with its Edit link. Below that, Make it stand out is optional. You can pick a Featured plan, or just list your event for free. Nothing is public until you tap Add my event. Your event then goes on the map. Next up: Show off your home."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Show off your home (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#showcase","text":"Show off your home. Put your lights on the map so families can find your home. You do not need a contest. In this video, you will put your own home on the map, so families can find your lights and drive by. You will add the address, a description, and photos. You need to be signed in to add a home, and in this video we already are. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Now look at the top of the map, beside the search box. See your picture, or your initial, in a circle? That is your account menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site. Tap it. In the menu, tap Add an event, home or contest. That is where you add anything new: an event, a contest, or your own home. Now tap A lighting display."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Show off your home (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#showcase","text":"This is for showing off your own home, or a home you decorated for a customer, so families can drive by and see it. Who is posting? A homeowner is for your own home. A professional installer is for a home your company did. We are a homeowner, so tap A homeowner, then Next. Type the name to show. This is the name people see on the map, for example, the Garcia family. It does not have to be your full name. Did a company install your lights? If one did, you can name them, and your home links to their profile. We did it ourselves, so tap No, then Next. Which season are your lights for? Visitors pick a season on the map, so choose the one your lights belong to. Tap Christmas, then Next. Type a display name, and tell us what makes your home special."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Show off your home (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#showcase","text":"Is it the music, the animation, the number of lights? Families read this to decide whether it is worth the drive. Add the address. This is where the pin goes, and visitors see it so they can drive by, so only add an address you want people to visit. Tell families when the lights are on, and how to enjoy them, like drive by, walk by, or where to park. Good directions make for happier visitors, and happier neighbors. Add photos. Take them at night, with the lights on. The first photo is the cover people see on the map. Finally, check everything, and tap Put it on the map. Your home usually goes on the map right away, for any family in Southern California to find. If it is held for a quick check, it appears once the site owner has looked it over. Next up: Find lights near me."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Claim your home (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#claim","text":"Claim your home. Is this your home? Replace the automatic listing with your own photos and details. In this video, you will claim a home that is already on the map, so your own photos and details replace the automatic listing. You need to be signed in to do this, and in this video we already are. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Say you spot your own home on the map, listed from a public list of homes. Search for your street, and open your home's card. Homes we found on public lists have a link at the bottom of the card. Tap Is this your home? Claim it. The form opens with your address already filled in, and tells you which listing you are claiming. Tap A homeowner, then Next."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Claim your home (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#claim","text":"Type the name to show, like the Park family, then tap Next. If a company installed your lights, you can name them here. We did it ourselves, so tap No, then Next. Tap Christmas, then Next. The name is filled in from the listing. Change it if you like, and tell families what makes your lights special. Then tap Next. Check the address. It is already filled in, so just tap Next. Telling families when your lights are on is optional. Tap Next. Add photos of your own lights, taken at night. They replace the picture on the automatic listing. Finally, check everything, and tap Put it on the map. Your home usually goes live at once, and it replaces the automatic listing at that address. If it is held for a quick check, it goes live once the site owner has looked it over. That is it."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Claim your home (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#claim","text":"Your home usually goes live at once, and it replaces the automatic listing, with your own photos and details. Next up: Feature your event or home."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Feature your event or home (1 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#feature","text":"Feature your event or home. Pick a plan, pick your event or winning home, check the dates. Stops at the secure payment page. In this video, you will see how to feature an event, or a winning home, so it stands out on the map. We go as far as the secure payment page, and stop there. We start on the map. Start at SoCal Lights dot app. On the home page, tap Use my location. Then tap Allow. Look at the top right corner of the map, and tap the three dots. In the menu, tap Promote your event. Listing an event is always free. This is for organizers who want to stand out. First, pick a plan. There are four, and a table below the cards compares everything each one includes. Every plan includes the gold Featured pin on the map."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Feature your event or home (2 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#feature","text":"The banner adds your event as a Featured pill, in the strip at the top of the map list. And Top of the list puts your event first when people browse your area. For prices, see the Pricing page. Tap Choose on the plan you want. We will take the banner with the pin. Next, pick what to feature. This list shows your events, and homes that finished first, second or third in an announced contest. Is your event already on the map, but not on your account? Open Claim an existing listing, find it by name or city, and tap This is my event: claim it. If your email matches the event's own website, it is approved at once. Otherwise we check it and email you. Tap your event. This page shows exactly what you get, when it starts, when it ends, and the total, before you pay. Then tap Pay on Stripe."},{"title":"Video tutorial: Feature your event or home (3 of 3)","url":"/tutorials#feature","text":"This opens Stripe's secure page, where you pay with a card or Cash App Pay. We stop here, because this is only a demonstration, and we never enter card details in a video. There is no subscription. If your event is cancelled, email us, and we will move your placement to another of your events or refund the unused time. A winning home works the same way. That is how you feature an event or a winning home. Listing is always free, and a Featured placement is optional. Next up: Find lights near me."},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Pricing","url":"/pricing","text":"Finding lights and events is free, and so is posting them. Paid options only make your listing easier to see. Every paid placement is labelled, and money never changes a contest result."},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Homeowners","url":"/pricing","text":"Free Show your home's lights on the map. Adding your home and entering a contest are free. A contest winner, 2nd or 3rd place can also feature their home for 30 days: Featured pin Top of the list Add your display"},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Contest organizers","url":"/pricing","text":"Contest organizers choose a pass for their contests. Cities, HOAs, chambers and neighborhood groups: resident entries, voting, judges, and People's Choice and Judges' Choice awards. Residents enter and vote for free. A pass only counts contests. Passes are chosen when you create a contest, or any time on the contest pass page . A pass is one payment on Stripe's secure page and never renews by itself. Your 12-month window starts the day you buy your pass (or the day your first contest goes live, if that comes first), and upgrading inside it costs only the difference. Passes are for the organization: the website matching your email's domain, or your own account."},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Event organizers","url":"/pricing","text":"Listing your event is free (tree lighting, parade, market, haunted house, light show). Featured placement is optional: pick one, pay once, no subscription. Gold-ringed pin Your event stands out on the map. “Featured” pill in the strip Shown at the top of the map list whenever your event is in view (banner plans). “Featured” badge On your listing in the list and on its page. Listed first Above other places when people browse your area (top of the list plans). Free"},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Your listing","url":"/pricing","text":"A pin on the map and a card in the list. Add your event Choose a plan, sign in with an emailed code, pick your event, and pay on Stripe's secure page. The exact dates and total are shown before you pay."},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Installers","url":"/pricing","text":"Show your company on the map and in your homes' pages. Paid placements are always labelled \"Sponsor\". Free"},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Profile card","url":"/pricing","text":"Your company page at socallights.app/installer/your-company: name, areas served, phone, website, an About blurb and every home you installed, with a Request a quote button. It is linked from each of those homes on the map. Create your free profile You pay on Stripe's secure page, one time for the season by default. Automatic renewal is optional: it is off unless you tick the box on the payment step (or turn it on in your dashboard), and you can cancel it any time. You can also renew or upgrade by hand from your dashboard. Questions? Email info@socallights.app ."},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Good to know (1 of 4)","url":"/pricing","text":"What does \"season\" mean? A placement runs from the moment you pay until your event ends or the season ends (Christmas lights: Jan 6), whichever comes first. The price is the same whenever you buy in a season, so the exact end date is shown before you pay. How do I pay, and do I get a receipt? You pay on Stripe's secure page with a card or Cash App Pay. Stripe emails the receipt, and we send a confirmation. There is no subscription for event plans. Can I get a refund? If we cannot list or feature your event for any reason, you get a full refund. Once your placement is live, payments are non-refundable, but if your event is cancelled, email us and we will move your placement to another of your events or refund the unused time. See our Terms . Can I add a part later? Yes."},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Good to know (2 of 4)","url":"/pricing","text":"Each part (the Featured pin, the banner, top of the list) has the same price: if you already have some of them, you pay only for the parts you add. Can paying change who wins a contest? No. Contests are free to enter and free to vote in, and payment never affects who wins or any contest result. A home can only be featured after the winners are announced, and only if it finished winner, 2nd or 3rd place. Is a Featured event labelled? Yes. Featured events and homes carry a visible Featured badge, and the banner pill is labelled \"Featured\", so nobody is misled. They still have to be real, public and accurate. How do contest passes work? An organization chooses a pass before a contest goes live: a single contest, up to 5 contests or up to 20 contests in a 12-month window."},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Good to know (3 of 4)","url":"/pricing","text":"The window starts the day you buy the pass, or the day the organization's first contest goes live if that comes first. You can upgrade later by paying only the difference. A contest counts when it goes live. Drafts, contests held for review and contests that were never live do not count. When the window ends, your next contest needs a new pass. A pass counts contests only: entering and voting stay free, and payment never affects any result. We sometimes give a founding partner contests for a period; that organization sees \"Founding partner: free through\" a date and no price. Will finding lights ever cost money? Finding events and lights, search, routes, favorites and voting in contests are meant to stay free for everyone. That is what the site is for. Questions?"},{"title":"Pricing · SoCal Lights: Good to know (4 of 4)","url":"/pricing","text":"Email info@socallights.app ."},{"title":"Feature your event · SoCal Lights: Feature your event","url":"/feature","text":"A Featured event stands out on the SoCal Lights map with a gold Featured badge and a bigger gold-ringed pin. You can add a banner pill at the top of the map list, and top of the list. Listing an event is free; this is optional. See all pricing . Run several events a season as a city, chamber, nonprofit or event producer? See Event Partner . No payment was made. Your event and plan are still chosen below, so you can pick up where you left off. Event promotion isn't available in the app."},{"title":"Feature your event · SoCal Lights: Online payment isn't switched on yet","url":"/feature","text":"Email us your event's name and its link on the map and we'll set up your Featured placement by hand. If it is missing, add it for free first. Email us to get started"},{"title":"Feature your event · SoCal Lights: Good to know (1 of 3)","url":"/feature","text":"Is it labeled as an ad? Featured events and homes carry a visible Featured badge, and an event's banner pill at the top of the map list is labelled \"Featured\", so nobody is misled. We only list real, public events, and a Featured event still has to be accurate: dates, venue and address are checked like every other listing. What can I feature? Public Halloween, Día de los Muertos, Thanksgiving, Christmas and holiday, New Year's, spring, and summer events in Southern California: haunts, pumpkin patches, tree lightings, light shows, parades, markets and more. We reserve the right to decline events that are not open to the public or that we cannot verify. Can I get a refund?"},{"title":"Feature your event · SoCal Lights: Good to know (2 of 3)","url":"/feature","text":"If we cannot list or feature your event for any reason, you get a full refund, for every part of your plan: the pin, the banner and top of the list. Once your placement is live, payments are non-refundable, but if your event is cancelled, email us and we will move your placement to another of your events or refund the unused time. A refund switches your Featured placement off (the pin, the banner and top of the list), and so does a payment dispute, so please email info@socallights.app before disputing a charge with your bank. The same rules are in our Terms . How long does it run, and what does an upgrade cost? It runs from the moment you pay until your event ends or the season ends (Christmas lights: Jan 6), whichever comes first, and the price is the same whenever you buy."},{"title":"Feature your event · SoCal Lights: Good to know (3 of 3)","url":"/feature","text":"Each part (the Featured pin, the banner, top of the list) has the same price: if you already have some of them, you pay only for the parts you add, and a purchase that adds nothing new is not sold. This page shows the exact dates and the total before you pay. Does Featured change my event's details? No. Your event keeps its dates, hours and link. Send us corrections any time at no charge."},{"title":"Feature your event · SoCal Lights: Questions?","url":"/feature","text":"Email us SoCal Lights is a small independent project. Thanks for helping keep it free for everyone."},{"title":"Sponsor placement for installers · SoCal Lights: Sponsor placement for installers","url":"/sponsor","text":"Put your company where people planning a drive look: the labelled Sponsor banner at the top of the list on the map, your company card at the top of the list, or both. Pay one time for the season. Automatic renewal is optional and off unless you tick the box before paying. The map and your free profile stay free, and money never changes a contest result. Thank you! Your payment went through. A receipt is on its way from Stripe. No payment was made. Your company and plan are still chosen below, so you can pick up where you left off. Paid placement isn't available in the app. Open socallights.app in your browser."},{"title":"Sponsor placement for installers · SoCal Lights: Online payment isn't switched on yet","url":"/sponsor","text":"Email info@socallights.app and we'll set up your placement by hand."},{"title":"Sponsor placement for installers · SoCal Lights: How it works (1 of 3)","url":"/sponsor","text":"How long does a season last, and what does it cost? Can I renew or upgrade? Renew: buy the same plan again while it is running and the new season starts the day after the current one ends, at the plan's price. Time is only ever added, and we sell at most one season ahead. Automatic renewal renews the same plan: a Top of the list placement renews as Top of the list. Upgrade: Banner and Top of the list are different products, and Banner and top of the list is both. If you have the Banner or Top of the list running to the end of the season and want both, choose Banner and top of the list: you pay the difference between that plan and the one you hold, the missing one starts today and runs to the end of the season, and what you have stays as it is."},{"title":"Sponsor placement for installers · SoCal Lights: How it works (2 of 3)","url":"/sponsor","text":"Buying the other single product instead is a full, separate purchase. This page shows the exact dates and price before you pay. Is it labelled as an ad? Yes. Paid placements are always labelled \"Sponsor\" in the banner and on the list card, and your profile still has to be real and accurate. Can I get a refund? If we cannot put your placement on for any reason, you get a full refund. Once it is running, payments are non-refundable, but email us if something is wrong. A refund switches the placement off, and so does a payment dispute, so please email us before disputing a charge with your bank. Does paying change a contest? No. Payment never affects who wins or any contest result, and finding lights and events on the map stays free for everyone. Questions? Email info@socallights.app ."},{"title":"Sponsor placement for installers · SoCal Lights: How it works (3 of 3)","url":"/sponsor","text":"Back to pricing ."},{"title":"Contest passes · SoCal Lights: Contest passes","url":"/contest-pass","text":"A contest pass lets your organization run a single contest, up to 5 or up to 20 inside a 12-month window. It only counts contests: entering and voting stay free, and payment never affects any result, vote, judge or ranking. Loading…"},{"title":"Add to SoCal Lights: What would you like to add?","url":"/add","text":"Pick one. Nothing goes public until the last step, and you can go back at any time. See pricing : what is free and what is optional."},{"title":"Remove my home · SoCal Lights: Remove my home","url":"/remove-home","text":"If your home is on SoCal Lights and you would rather it were not, tell us and we will take it off. You do not need an account. We will email you when it is done. Which home? The listing id from the page, or the street address Your email so we can confirm and tell you when it is removed Anything else we should know? (optional) Website Send my request We use your email only to answer this request. See the Privacy Policy . You can also write to info@socallights.app ."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Terms of Use","url":"/terms","text":"SoCal Lights (socallights.app), operated by Forza-TEK. Effective November 1, 2026. Updated November 11, 2026. Plain-language terms."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: The short version","url":"/terms","text":"SoCal Lights is a free map of holiday events and decorated homes. Use it kindly, post only true things and your own photos, and know that contests are run by organizers, not by us. Some optional paid placements are described under \"Paid placements\" below."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Using the site","url":"/terms","text":"You must be 13 or older to sign in. To enter a home in a contest you must be 18 or older. Events and times come from public sources and from people who post them. They can change or be wrong. Check with the organizer before you go. We don't guarantee any event will happen. Visit displays from the street. Don't go onto private property, block driveways, or disturb neighbors. You are responsible for your own safety and your children's. Don't misuse the site: no scraping that slows it down, no fake accounts, no attempts to break in, no spam."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: What you post","url":"/terms","text":"Post only events and homes that are real, and photos you took or have permission to use. Don't post photos that show identifiable people, especially children. When you post an event, a home or a contest entry, it is shown publicly on the site and map with the details you give, including a home's street address. You can ask us to remove it, or withdraw a contest entry yourself. We may remove or edit anything that is wrong, unsafe, spam or breaks these terms."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contests (1 of 4)","url":"/terms#contests","text":"Each contest is run by its organizer (the \"Sponsor\"), who writes the official rules, decides who wins, and provides and is responsible for any prize. SoCal Lights provides the website only. It is not a sponsor and is not responsible for any prize or for any dispute between an organizer and an entrant. Entering and voting are free. Votes are never sold. Fake, automated or traded votes are not allowed, and an organizer or we may void them. Organizers agree to run their contest fairly, follow the law that applies to it, honor the prizes they promise, and not charge entrants to enter or vote. Sponsors collected by the organizer."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contests (2 of 4)","url":"/terms#contests","text":"An organizer may record a sponsor who paid them, and show its logo, name and link on the contest page, labelled \"Sponsor\", once the organizer confirms they collected the payment themselves (or that the sponsorship was donated in kind), have the sponsor's permission, and choose to show it. Sponsorship payments go to the organizer, not to SoCal Lights. The organizer is responsible for collecting, receipting and reporting sponsorship money. SoCal Lights does not process, hold or guarantee it and is not a party to it. The organizer must have the sponsor's permission to list their name, logo and contact details. Sponsors do not influence results."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contests (3 of 4)","url":"/terms#contests","text":"We keep the sponsor's contact details and any receipt the organizer uploads privately (visible only to the contest team and SoCal Lights staff), and we may hide a sponsor from a contest page. Sponsor invite links. An organizer of a live contest that we have approved may share a private sponsor invite link and give sponsors their own payment link or instructions. A sponsor who uses the link sends the organizer a request; nothing is shown on the contest page until the organizer confirms the payment (or an in-kind gift) and approves it. The organizer's payment link and instructions are the organizer's own: we do not verify them, and a sponsor should pay only an organizer they know. We never take, hold or pass on sponsorship money."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contests (4 of 4)","url":"/terms#contests","text":"We may switch off an invite link, and hide a payment link or text, or a sponsor, at any time. Sponsors and installers shown on a contest page are identified as such. If you ask an installer for a quote, your details go only to that installer and the organizer, and only if you tick the box."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Paid placements","url":"/terms#paid","text":"Finding lights and events, listing an event or a home, entering a contest and voting are free. Paid placements are optional and only make a listing easier to see; contest organizers buy a pass to run contests (see Contest organizer passes below). Prices and the exact start and end dates are shown on our Pricing page and on the Feature page before you pay."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Featured events and homes (1 of 4)","url":"/terms","text":"What it is. A Featured placement gives an event or a home a gold-ringed pin on the map and a \"Featured\" badge in the list and on its details. For an event it is sold in parts: the Featured pin (always included in any paid plan), a banner (the event shows as a pill labelled \"Featured\" in the strip at the top of the map list, with its name, city and dates, while it is in the map view) and top of the list (the event is listed first when people browse the area). The plans are the pin, the banner with the pin, top of the list with the pin, and all three, at the prices on our Pricing page. A home can be featured with the pin or with top of the list. Event Partner. An organization (such as a city, chamber of commerce, nonprofit or event producer) can buy an Event Partner plan for a season."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Featured events and homes (2 of 4)","url":"/terms","text":"It covers up to 10 of that organization's events in the season with the Featured pin for each event's run window, and shows an \"Event Partner\" badge on those events. The badge shows only that the organization has the plan; it is not an endorsement by SoCal Lights. The refund, dispute and removal rules in this section apply to it, and a refund or a lost dispute switches off the pins the plan applied. How you pay. You pay once, by card on Stripe's payment page. There is no subscription and nothing renews by itself. We never see your card details. Stripe emails your receipt. Always labelled. A paid placement always carries the \"Featured\" badge, and an event's banner pill is labelled \"Featured\" (for installers, the \"Sponsor\" label described below)."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Featured events and homes (3 of 4)","url":"/terms","text":"A paid placement does not change the details of the listing, and the listing still has to be real and accurate. Contests. Payment never affects any contest result or any vote. A home can be featured only after the contest winners are announced, only if it finished winner, 2nd or 3rd place, and only by the home's owner (or, for a home the organizer added, the organizer who runs that contest). When it runs. A placement starts when your payment goes through. For an event it ends on the event's last day or on the last day of the season it was bought in (Christmas lights: Jan 6), whichever comes first. The price is flat: it does not depend on when you buy within the season, and the exact start and end dates and the total are shown before you pay."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Featured events and homes (4 of 4)","url":"/terms","text":"If you already have part of a plan, you pay only for the parts you add, and a purchase that adds nothing new is not sold. For a home it runs for 30 days from payment. Featuring opens 30 days before an event starts, and an event that has ended, been cancelled or been postponed cannot be featured. Buying again adds time and never shortens a placement you already have. Events waiting for review. Most events go live right away, but we may hold one for review. If you pay for an event that is waiting, the placement switches on when we approve it. No guarantee of results. We do not promise any number of views, visits, clicks or other results from a placement."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Installer Sponsor placements (1 of 3)","url":"/terms","text":"Installers can buy a Banner (a strip at the top of the map list), Top of the list (a company card at the top of the list, without the banner) or both together. These are always labelled \"Sponsor\". That label is separate from a contest's organizer, who is called the \"Sponsor\" in the Contests section above. They are paid per season on Stripe as a one-time payment by default. Automatic renewal is optional and off unless you tick the box (see below); Featured events and homes never renew. A placement runs from payment to the last day of the current season on the map, and the price is the plan's price shown on the Pricing page. The exact price, start date and end date are shown again before you pay. Automatic renewal (optional)."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Installer Sponsor placements (2 of 3)","url":"/terms","text":"It is off unless you tick the box on the payment step or turn it on in your installer dashboard. If you do, Stripe saves your card (we never see the card number) and we charge it on the last day of your placement, at the plan's price shown on the Pricing page at that time, so that the next season starts the next day. We email you 7 days before each charge with the amount and date. You can cancel before the charge from your dashboard or from the link in any email, with no login needed; cancelling keeps the placement you already paid for and removes the saved card. If a charge fails, we email you a link to pay or change your card and try once more 3 days later; if that fails too, automatic renewal stops and nothing more is charged automatically."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Installer Sponsor placements (3 of 3)","url":"/terms","text":"A refund or a payment dispute of an automatic charge pauses automatic renewal. We sell at most one season ahead. Only the verified owner of an installer profile can buy one. The refund, dispute, removal and no-guarantee rules in this section apply to these placements too."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contest organizer passes (1 of 4)","url":"/terms#passes","text":"What a pass is. An organization buys a pass before a contest goes live. A pass sets the total number of contests the organization may run in a 12-month window: a single contest, up to 5 contests or up to 20 contests. For more than 20 contests, write to info@socallights.app . The prices are on our Pricing page, and the exact window dates and the price are shown again before you pay. A pass counts contests only. It does not change what a contest includes, and it does not limit entries or votes. Who the organization is. It is the website of the contest when that website matches the domain of the organizer's verified email (for example, an address at the city's own website), and otherwise the organizer's own account. Co-organizers and staff do not create separate organizations."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contest organizer passes (2 of 4)","url":"/terms#passes","text":"We may combine or correct an organization by hand. The 12-month window. It starts on the day the organization buys its pass, or the day its first contest goes live if that comes first, and ends the day before the same date a year later. A contest counts when it goes live. A draft, a contest held for our review, a declined contest and a contest that was never live do not count. A contest that was live and later unpublished still counts. When the window ends, the next contest needs a new pass. Contests that were already live or announced before contest passes began are not counted and do not need a pass. A pass belongs to the window it was bought in, and you can upgrade inside the window by paying only the difference between the two passes. How you pay."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contest organizer passes (3 of 4)","url":"/terms#passes","text":"You pay once, by card on Stripe's payment page. A pass never renews by itself. We never see your card details. Before a contest goes live without a pass that has room for it, we ask you to choose a pass first, and we create the contest after the payment goes through. Refunds and disputes. If we cannot apply your pass, you get a full refund. If a pass is refunded, or a payment dispute is lost, before the extra contests it paid for went live, the pass is switched off. If those contests are already live, we review it by hand: the contests stay live and we decide what is fair. To ask for a refund or for help, write to info@socallights.app . Nothing here limits any refund right you have by law. Comps."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Contest organizer passes (4 of 4)","url":"/terms#passes","text":"We may give an organization contests without charge for a period, for example a founding partner. That organization sees that its contests are free through a date and is not shown a price. Payment never affects results. Entering and voting stay free for everyone. Buying a pass, or being given one, never affects any vote, judge, ranking, award or contest result."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: What we can do","url":"/terms","text":"We may decline an event, home or placement, and we may remove or unpublish a listing or its placement, if the listing is inaccurate, the event is cancelled, or it breaks these terms. Removing or unpublishing a listing switches its placement off with it."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Refunds and disputes (1 of 2)","url":"/terms","text":"If we cannot list or feature your event, for any reason, you get a full refund. This applies to every part of a plan: the pin, the banner and top of the list. Once your placement is live, payments are non-refundable. If your event is cancelled, email us and we will move your placement to another of your events or refund the unused time. A refund, or a lost payment dispute, switches the placement it paid for off: the pin, the banner and top of the list each end with the order that bought them, and time another paid order still covers stays. We make refunds ourselves through Stripe. To ask for one, or for any help with a payment, write to info@socallights.app . A refund switches your placement off, and so does a payment dispute (a chargeback with your bank)."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Refunds and disputes (2 of 2)","url":"/terms","text":"Please email us before disputing a charge. Nothing here limits any refund right you have by law."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Accounts","url":"/terms","text":"You sign in with Google or an emailed code. Keep your email account secure. You can delete your account any time from your profile. We may suspend accounts that break these terms."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (1 of 2)","url":"/terms#ai-assistant","text":"When it is turned on, the \"AI assistant\" is an automated program, not a person, and it is for information only. It can be wrong or out of date. Prices on the Pricing page and the rules in these Terms and on each contest page control over anything it says. It does not give legal, safety or medical advice, when it explains contests it only repeats our help pages and these Terms and does not interpret rules, decide disputes, eligibility questions or prizes, or predict results (each contest's official rules and its organizer control), and what it says does not create a contract, a promise of a refund or approval, or a promise that an event, date or home will be available. For a firm answer use the Help Center or email info@socallights.app ."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (2 of 2)","url":"/terms#ai-assistant","text":"Paying for anything on this site never affects an answer from the assistant or any contest result. Buttons it shows act only when you tap them, using the same rules and limits as the matching page on the site, and you are responsible for what you send. It cannot make payments, vote, change contest results or act on anyone else's account. Do not type personal information into your questions; its buttons ask only for what they need. We may limit or block use of it that looks like abuse, or turn it off at any time. How it handles what you type is described in the Privacy Policy ."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: No warranty, and limits on our responsibility","url":"/terms","text":"The site is provided as it is, without promises that it will always work or be accurate. To the extent the law allows, SoCal Lights and Forza-TEK are not responsible for losses that come from using the site, attending an event, visiting a display, or taking part in a contest, and our total responsibility to you for anything about the site is limited to $100. Some places don't allow these limits, so they may not apply to you."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Law","url":"/terms","text":"These terms are governed by California law. Any dispute will be handled in the courts of California."},{"title":"Terms of Use · SoCal Lights: Changes and contact","url":"/terms","text":"If we change these terms, we will update the date above. Questions or removal requests: info@socallights.app . See also our Privacy Policy ."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Privacy Policy","url":"/privacy","text":"SoCal Lights (socallights.app), operated by Forza-TEK. Effective November 1, 2026. Updated November 11, 2026. New on November 8, 2026: an optional AI assistant (a chat box) may be turned on. It sends what you type to Cloudflare, our hosting provider, so it can write an answer; we do not save the text. See AI assistant below. SoCal Lights is a free map of Halloween, Christmas and other seasonal events and neighbor-decorated homes in Southern California. You do not need an account to browse the map; you sign in to add an event or a home, and to run, enter or vote in a home decorating contest, because each listing, contest and vote belongs to your account. This page explains what information the website and the SoCal Lights apps handle, and what they do not."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: What we do not do (1 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"We do not use passwords, and you never need an account to browse the map. You sign in (with Google or an emailed code) to add an event or a home, or to run, enter or vote in a contest. We do not sell your personal information to data brokers, and we do not use advertising cookies or ad networks. A thin banner on the map, labelled \"Sponsor\", shows installers whose service area is in view. We count how many times each one is shown and tapped, as daily totals only. Nothing about you is recorded or shared with the installer. We do show some paid and commissioned placements. Events marked \"Featured\" are paid placements; sponsors and the presenting installer on a contest page are listed as sponsors; and Shop links to Amazon earn us a commission. They are labelled where they appear."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: What we do not do (2 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"The companies behind them do not receive your information from us unless you send them a quote request (see below). We do not build profiles of you or follow you across other apps or websites. The services listed under \"Who receives your data\" can see your connection details when your browser loads something from them."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Your location","url":"/privacy","text":"If you tap the location button, or choose \"My location\" as the starting point for a route, your device may ask to share its location. Your location is used on your device to center the map and to estimate drive times, and is not sent to our servers for that. The one exception is the \"I saw it lit tonight\" button on a home: when you tap it, your device's position is sent to our server once, only to check whether you are within about a quarter mile of that home. You can decline to share your location, and everything still works (the report just does not count as seen on site). When you open a route, your maps app (Google Maps or Apple Maps) receives the destinations, under its own privacy policy."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Information you choose to submit","url":"/privacy","text":"If you use Add an event or Showcase a house , we receive what you type in the form: Events: event name, web page link, dates, hours, price, venue, street address, description and your email address. Home displays: your name or business name, email address, street address, display description and photos. Professional installers may also add a website, phone number and service area. The event or home details, including the street address and photos, are shown publicly on the map. Your email address is private and is used only to contact you if something needs fixing. We store submissions and photos with Cloudflare. To ask for your event or house to be edited or removed, email info@socallights.app ."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Weekly lights email (1 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"If you sign up for the weekly email, we store your email address, the city and distance you chose, and the date we last emailed you, only to send that email. We send a confirmation link first and nothing else is sent until you tap it. We also keep the approximate coordinates of that city and a private link code for your unsubscribe link. Each email has a link that deletes your address immediately. We do not sell or share these addresses; the emails are sent through our email provider, Resend. Reminder, invitation and result emails about contests have their own unsubscribe link, which stops those emails but not the weekly email (and the other way round). Those emails also carry our postal address."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Weekly lights email (2 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"You can also sign up for the weekly email from the city and event pages, where a small box asks for just your email address. It works exactly as described above: we send a confirmation link first, nothing else is sent until you tap it, and every email has a one-tap unsubscribe link that deletes your address."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Monthly update (optional)","url":"/privacy","text":"If you tick \"Email me a monthly update with seasonal recommendations\", in your profile or on the last step of adding an event, home or contest, we store your email address, the role you chose (installer, event or contest host, or family), the topics you picked, when and where you agreed, and which wording you saw. The box is never ticked for you, and having an account or a listing does not sign you up. You can change your role and topics or unsubscribe from your profile or from the preferences link in each email, without signing in; every email also has a one-click unsubscribe. Sponsored or affiliate content in the update will be identified as such. If you unsubscribe, we keep the record for 12 months as proof, and your address goes on the do-not-contact list described below."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Contacting businesses and organizations, and opt-outs (1 of 3)","url":"/privacy","text":"We may email businesses and organizations (for example lighting installers, contest organizers and seasonal-event hosts) at addresses published on their own websites or other public sources, to tell them about SoCal Lights. Those emails are sent from our own mailbox, identify us and include a way to opt out. We do not buy lists. Before each message our outreach tool asks the app whether the address belongs to someone who already has an account or a listing, has opted out, or is on the do-not-contact list, and it receives only a status word back, never any of your details."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Contacting businesses and organizations, and opt-outs (2 of 3)","url":"/privacy","text":"For a business address, our tool may also ask Cloudflare's public DNS service (cloudflare-dns.com) whether the address's domain name (the part after the @, never the rest of the address) can receive mail; Cloudflare sees that domain name, and we keep the answer for 24 hours. To honor opt-outs, we keep a do-not-contact list that stores only a one-way hash (a scrambled code) of an email address, with the reason (for example a reply, unsubscribe, bounce or complaint), where it came from, and the date. It does not store the address itself, so it cannot be turned back into the address. An address goes on it when someone asks us to stop, unsubscribes from any SoCal Lights marketing email, or deletes their account."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Contacting businesses and organizations, and opt-outs (3 of 3)","url":"/privacy","text":"We also keep hashed counts of our outreach messages and replies, to see whether outreach works, for 24 months. To be added or to ask us about this, email info@socallights.app ."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (1 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"Creating a home decorating contest, entering a home in one, or voting in one requires signing in. You can sign in with Google or with a one-time code that we email to you. We never ask you to create a password. What we keep: your email address, your name if Google shares it, and your sign-in sessions. If you use Google, we receive only your email address and name; we do not receive your Google password and do not read any other Google data. Sign-in cookie: a cookie keeps you signed in on your device for up to 30 days. Signing out removes it. Contest organizers can see the name, email address, street address and photos of people who enter their contest, so they can run it."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (2 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"Homes added by an organizer: a contest organizer may add homes on residents' behalf, using addresses the residents gave the organizer, and must confirm that those residents agreed to have their address shown on the public map. We keep a record of that confirmation (the organizer, the date, the contest, how many homes and the exact wording they confirmed). The map then shows the address like any other entry, and an organizer-added entry is not linked to any account. See also \"Homes listed by our team or by contest organizers\" below. Votes are stored with your account so that each person can vote once. They are not shown publicly with your name."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (3 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"To catch cheating, a contest's organizers (and their co-organizers) can review each vote with the voter's email address, and can void a vote that looks fake. Contest emails: if you enter or vote, we may email you when your entry is approved or declined, when voting opens and closes, and when winners are announced. Co-organizers a contest owner adds can see the same organizer information. Quote requests: this is optional and separate from entering or voting. Some contests name a presenting installer, and installers have profile pages. If you tick the box on a quote form, we send the details you typed (your name, your sign-in email address, your phone number if you entered one, and your note) to the installer named on the form, and on a contest page also to that contest's organizer."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (4 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"We keep a copy, which the contest organizer can see in their contest dashboard, or the installer in their profile dashboard when you asked from an installer's page. The installer and organizer use the details to respond to you and are responsible for what they do with them. We do not send quote details to anyone you did not name on the form, and nothing is sent unless you tick the box. Visit counts: a contest page counts visits (and visits from its QR code) as totals only, with no personal information. Sponsors recorded by organizers: an organizer may record a sponsor who paid them: the sponsor's company, contact name, website, phone, email, logo, an optional amount, how and when the organizer was paid, and an optional receipt or paid invoice."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (5 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"The contest page shows only the logo, name and website of a sponsor the organizer chose to show, with that sponsor's permission. The contact details, amount and receipt are visible only to that contest's organizers and co-organizers and to our staff, and the receipt file is stored privately, never in a public list. We keep them until 24 months after the contest's voting ends, or until the organizer removes the record or deletes their account, and then delete them. Sponsorship money goes to the organizer, not to us; we do not process or hold it."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (6 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"A sponsor can also fill in a form from an organizer's private invite link, with no account: we collect the same details, run the \"I am human\" check, keep a one-way scrambled code (not the address itself) of the connection for two days to limit abuse, and email the sponsor a short confirmation. Nothing from that form is shown on a page until the organizer approves it. The organizer may also give sponsors their own payment link or instructions; we show it on that private page and have not verified it. Deleting your account: open your profile, choose the Account tab and tap Delete my account. We delete your account, sign-ins, saved drafts, profile picture, contest entries and votes, close any contests you run, and remove your email from homes and events you posted."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (7 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"We also remove your email, name and phone from judge invitations and judge ballots, co-organizer lists, edit histories, event claims, your weekly email signup and contact details on reports, and from our copy of quote requests you sent (the installer or organizer already has the email we sent them, and we cannot take that back). If you run an installer profile, we remove your email from it and delete the quote requests it received. A few things stay: order records for Featured placements (see \"Payments on the website\", with your account link removed), and the record that an address unsubscribed, so we do not email it again. A contest whose results were already announced keeps its results, with your home removed from them."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Accounts, contests and voting (8 of 8)","url":"/privacy","text":"You can also email info@socallights.app from the address you signed in with. Optional profile details: you can add a name, a home address and a profile picture to your profile. They are used only to fill in forms for you and to show your picture to you, and are never shown publicly. If you sign in with Google we save the picture Google provides until you upload your own."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Homes listed by our team or by contest organizers (1 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"Not every home on the map was added by the people who live there. Our team may list a home from a public source, for example a city, school or community holiday tour or contest page, and a contest organizer may add homes to their own contest. Such a listing can show the home's street address, a name if the public source gave one (for example a family name in the display's title), and sometimes a photo taken from the same public page or supplied by the organizer. We do not list a private home's address unless a public page or the organizer provided it. If you live at a listed home and want it removed, use the \"Not your listing? Remove my home\" link on the listing, or the Remove my home form, or email info@socallights.app . You do not need an account."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Homes listed by our team or by contest organizers (2 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"Tell us the address (or the listing id) and an email address where we can reach you. We reply to confirm we have the request, and once it is approved, normally within two business days, we delete the listing and any photos we hold and email you. Search engines and people who saved the page may keep a copy for a while, which we do not control. We use your email only to answer the request."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Spam protection and technical data (1 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"To see which pages are useful, we count page views: one number per page per day (for example, \"the Tutorials page was opened 40 times today\"). The count has no IP address, no cookie, no account and no way to tell who looked. We also count taps on certain links and buttons, such as event ticket links, directions, shop links and the Promote your event button, as one total per day. These counts have no IP address, no cookie, no account and no way to tell who tapped. Forms use Cloudflare Turnstile to tell people from bots. To limit abuse, we briefly record the network (IP) address of each form submission, report or help message, and of each sign-in code we send. These records are deleted automatically within two days."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Spam protection and technical data (2 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"Like any website, our hosting provider, Cloudflare, processes standard connection data such as IP address and browser type to deliver the site and keep it secure."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (1 of 6)","url":"/privacy#ai-assistant","text":"When it is turned on, a chat button labelled \"AI assistant\" appears on some pages. It is an automated program, not a person, and its answers can be wrong. Use the Pricing page, the Help Center and the Terms for firm answers, or email info@socallights.app . Buttons that do things. The assistant can show buttons under an answer, such as sending a message to our team, signing you up for the weekly email, asking us to remove a home, reporting a problem with a listing, or opening a form with details filled in. Nothing is sent until you fill in the button's boxes and tap it, and only what is in those boxes is sent. If you choose Send to the team, the text you wrote, your email address if you gave one, and the page you were on are emailed to us and saved with our other reports so we can reply."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (2 of 6)","url":"/privacy#ai-assistant","text":"We do not save the rest of your chat. For each kind of button we keep only a daily count of how many were shown and used, never what anyone wrote. What is sent: the question you type and the last few messages of that chat, together with public information from our own site that matches your question (help articles, tutorials, the Terms and this policy, the price list, upcoming events, and the names, dates and status of live contests; never votes, results, accounts or anyone's contact details). Nothing else about you is sent: no account, name, email address or location. Who receives it: Cloudflare Workers AI, which acts as our service provider and writes the answer."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (3 of 6)","url":"/privacy#ai-assistant","text":"According to Cloudflare, it does not keep the content of messages sent for a standard answer and does not use them to train its models. If we ever switch the assistant to Claude from Anthropic, the same text is sent to Anthropic instead; according to Anthropic it does not train its models on this kind of use by default and deletes the text from its systems within 30 days (longer only where safety or law requires). We do not use your chats to train any model. What we keep: we do not save what you type or what the assistant answers on our servers, and we do not log your IP address for it."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (4 of 6)","url":"/privacy#ai-assistant","text":"So that your conversation is still there when you move to another page of the site, your browser keeps it in that browser tab only (session storage); it is erased when you close the tab or tap \"Start over\", and it is never sent anywhere except as part of a new message you send. We keep only anonymous daily totals (how many chats and questions, how much text was processed, how many were refused or stopped). When our help pages have nothing on a question, we add one to a count under a general topic label chosen from a fixed list (for example \"accounts\" or \"contest-setup\"), so we know what help to write; the label does not contain your words."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (5 of 6)","url":"/privacy#ai-assistant","text":"To limit abuse we use the \"I am human\" check once per visit, a short-lived cookie that only says you passed it (about 30 minutes, no name or account in it), and one-way scrambled codes of your connection (not the address itself) kept for two days to count how many questions one connection has asked. The conversation itself stays in your browser window and is gone when you close or reload the page. Please do not type personal information (names, addresses, phone numbers, email addresses or card numbers). The assistant does not need it, will not look up information about people, and refuses questions that ask for it. Paying never changes an answer or a contest result. The assistant explains how contests, entering and voting work by quoting our help pages and Terms."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: AI assistant (6 of 6)","url":"/privacy#ai-assistant","text":"It does not interpret rules, settle disputes or predict results; it points to the Terms and our email address instead."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Information stored on your device","url":"/privacy","text":"The site remembers a few choices on your device only: your saved places in Favorites, your day or night display choice, your preferred maps app, and whether you have hidden the Favorites tips. You can clear these by clearing the site's data in your browser."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Links to other sites and affiliate links","url":"/privacy","text":"Event pages, ticket sellers, maps apps (Google Maps, Apple Maps, Waze), Google Calendar and Amazon are separate services with their own privacy policies. Some links in the Shop section are Amazon affiliate links: if you buy something, we may earn a small commission at no cost to you. Amazon, not SoCal Lights, receives any information from that visit."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Who receives your data (1 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"These are the companies and services that handle information on our behalf or that your browser contacts when you use the site. We do not sell your information to any of them. Cloudflare: hosts the site, stores the database and photos, runs the Turnstile spam check, processes connection data (IP address, browser type), and, through Workers AI, writes the answers of the optional AI assistant from what you type into it (see \"AI assistant\"). Anthropic: only if we switch the AI assistant to Claude: it then receives what you type into the assistant instead of Cloudflare (see \"AI assistant\"). Resend: sends our emails, so it receives the recipient address and the email's contents. Stripe: processes payments for Featured and Sponsor placements; you enter card details on Stripe's page, not ours."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Who receives your data (2 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"If you opt in to automatic renewal of a Sponsor placement, Stripe also keeps your card for the later charges; we store only Stripe's customer and payment-method ids, never the card number, and we ask Stripe to remove the card when you cancel or delete your account. Google: sign-in, if you choose \"Continue with Google\"; and Google Fonts, which supplies the site's typeface (your browser asks Google for the font files, so Google sees your IP address and browser type). Geoapify: address suggestions. When you type in an address box, the text you typed is sent from our server to Geoapify to suggest matches. U.S. Census Bureau geocoder and OpenStreetMap (Nominatim): when you submit an event or home, its street address is sent from our server to one of these to find its map position."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Who receives your data (3 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"OpenFreeMap: supplies the map tiles. Your browser fetches them directly, so it sees your IP address and the part of the map you view. Map software: the open-source MapLibre library that draws the map is served from our own site, so loading it does not send your visit to another company. Event image hosts: pictures on some event listings load directly from the event organizers' own websites, which see your IP address when a picture loads. YouTube (privacy-enhanced mode) and Vimeo: only on the Tutorials page, if a video is embedded there. Amazon: Shop links; only if you click one. Installers and contest organizers: only the quote or contest details described above. Cash App: only if you tap the optional tip button. Those companies may collect information about you under their own policies."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Who receives your data (4 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"We do not allow ad networks on our pages, and we cannot tell whether these services link what they see across different websites."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Do Not Track","url":"/privacy","text":"Our site does not change what it does when your browser sends a \"Do Not Track\" signal. What we collect is described on this page: what you submit, your account details if you sign in, a brief record of your IP address for spam protection, anonymous daily totals of page views and taps, and, if you use the optional AI assistant, what you type into it (sent to produce an answer and not saved by us; see \"AI assistant\"). We do not use advertising or cross-site tracking cookies and do not build profiles of your browsing."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: How long we keep information (1 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"Accounts: until you delete yours. We do not delete inactive accounts automatically. A sign-in session lasts up to 30 days; a sign-in code lasts 10 minutes. AI assistant: chat text is not saved on our servers. Your browser tab holds it until you close the tab or tap \"Start over\". Anonymous daily totals, including how many times each kind of button was shown and used, are kept for 120 days. A message you choose to send to our team is saved with our other reports. The scrambled connection codes used to limit questions are deleted after two days, and the 30-minute check cookie expires on its own. Events, homes and photos: until they are removed or you ask us to remove them. Weekly email signup: until you unsubscribe."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: How long we keep information (2 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"Quote requests: until you delete your account (your details are then removed from our copy), or the contest or installer profile that holds the copy is deleted. Sponsor records: contact details, amounts, payment notes and receipts are deleted 24 months after the contest's voting ends, when the organizer removes the record, or when the organizer deletes their account. Order records: financial records of Featured placements are kept as long as we need them for accounting, tax, refund and dispute purposes. They hold the event, plan, amount, your account email at the time, and Stripe's payment reference, and never card numbers."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: How long we keep information (3 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"Removal requests and organizer confirmations: a \"Remove my home\" request (the address or listing id, your email and note) is kept after it is handled as a record that we answered it. The record of an organizer's confirmation for homes they added is kept for as long as those homes are listed. Email limits: to stop abuse we keep a one-way scrambled code (not the address itself) of recent sign-ups and quote requests for two days, to count how many emails one account, address or connection has asked us to send. Unsubscribe records: the address and a private code, kept so that an address that opted out is not emailed again. Monthly update opt-in: until you unsubscribe or delete your account; the record of an unsubscribe is deleted 12 months later."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: How long we keep information (4 of 4)","url":"/privacy","text":"Do-not-contact list: the one-way hash of an address, kept so that it is not contacted again; it holds no address, name or other detail. Hashed outreach counts are deleted after 24 months."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Payments on the website (1 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"On the website, event hosts can pay to feature an event. Payments are handled by Stripe, and we never see or store card details. If you buy a Featured placement we keep the order (the event, the plan, the amount, your account email and Stripe's payment reference) as a financial record, so we can handle refunds and disputes and meet accounting and tax needs; deleting your account removes the link between the order and your account but not the order itself. Card details are handled by Stripe and never reach us. The optional tip button opens a Cash App page, and Cash App handles that payment under its own policy. Neither payments nor tips are offered in the store apps."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Payments on the website (2 of 2)","url":"/privacy","text":"If you tell us an event is yours (a claim) so that you can feature it, we keep the claim (the event and your account email) until you delete your account, and we email you once when we approve or decline it. That is a service email, not marketing."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Children","url":"/privacy","text":"SoCal Lights is a family-friendly guide but is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect information from them. See also our Terms of Use ."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Changes","url":"/privacy","text":"If we change this policy, we will update the \"Updated\" date at the top of this page. For a material change (for example a new kind of information collected or a new recipient), we will also say so at the top of this page and email people who have an account."},{"title":"Privacy Policy · SoCal Lights: Contact","url":"/privacy","text":"Questions or requests: info@socallights.app ."}];
